00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:36.633
lattes.

00:19:36.633 --> 00:19:39.233
Yes, we are fully Angelino, okay?

00:19:40.070 --> 00:19:41.800
I didn't think latte was Angelino.

00:19:41.800 --> 00:19:43.600
It's just a lot of milk.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:45.030
I like a latte.

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:46.560
Yeah, but it's a little fru-fru.

00:19:46.560 --> 00:19:47.920
Let's just be honest.

00:19:47.920 --> 00:19:49.990
Okay.
Cowboys drink black coffee.

00:19:49.990 --> 00:19:52.760
You see Clint Eastwood showing up,

00:19:52.760 --> 00:19:54.640
you know, at the campfire
and asking for a latte?

00:19:54.640 --> 00:19:55.620
Do you have cream?
Yeah.

00:19:55.620 --> 00:19:57.370
I mean, come on, get
with the program here.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.070
It's a little fru-fru,

00:19:59.070 --> 00:20:00.970
but I unashamedly enjoy it,

00:20:00.970 --> 00:20:02.760
but you gotta make sure
you get a good one.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.580
So we would look up and where's
the best place for a latte.

00:20:05.580 --> 00:20:07.080
This place happened to have donuts.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.073
Food really directed your trip.

00:20:09.073 --> 00:20:09.940
Food was,

00:20:09.940 --> 00:20:12.450
I was like, we both really like to eat

00:20:12.450 --> 00:20:14.740
and we both really like lattes,

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:16.130
and so we're gonna be happy.

00:20:16.130 --> 00:20:17.450
If you're well fed

00:20:17.450 --> 00:20:18.310
you're going to be happy

00:20:18.310 --> 00:20:20.110
even if you don't get
to do some attraction

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:22.180
that you thought you wanted to do.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:23.100
Rather,

00:20:23.100 --> 00:20:24.070
better to be well fed

00:20:24.070 --> 00:20:25.633
than to be well
entertained with your eyes.

00:20:25.633 --> 00:20:28.000
I think I would do the same thing.

00:20:28.000 --> 00:20:30.470
I'm not much of a sight seeing person,

00:20:30.470 --> 00:20:33.190
especially when there's an itinerary.

00:20:33.190 --> 00:20:34.520
Yeah.
I think

00:20:34.520 --> 00:20:36.127
I would prioritize the food too.

00:20:36.127 --> 00:20:37.680
And I,

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:41.463
it's funny, both of these trips
have done something to me.

00:20:44.061 --> 00:20:44.894
Gotten me to a place.

00:20:44.894 --> 00:20:45.727
I have a tendency,

00:20:45.727 --> 00:20:46.760
and I know you have the same tendency,

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:50.760
to be like, am I really enjoying
this as much as I should?

00:20:50.760 --> 00:20:54.300
Did I make the best decision
about where we went?

00:20:54.300 --> 00:20:55.660
Did I stop at the right places?

00:20:55.660 --> 00:20:56.880
What did I miss?

00:20:56.880 --> 00:20:58.550
Did I do enough research?

00:20:58.550 --> 00:20:59.720
Are we having as good a time

00:20:59.720 --> 00:21:01.050
as we should be having right now?

00:21:01.050 --> 00:21:02.200
They call that FOMO.

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:04.450
I constantly ask myself that question.

00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:06.160
In fact, Locke and I had a
really great conversation

00:21:06.160 --> 00:21:08.290
as we were walking on the Maine coastline.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:10.520
I was talking about how
I'm having a great time.

00:21:10.520 --> 00:21:11.750
This is beautiful,

00:21:11.750 --> 00:21:12.583
but I just wanna,

00:21:12.583 --> 00:21:13.416
like Locke and I really,

00:21:13.416 --> 00:21:14.360
we connect a lot,

00:21:14.360 --> 00:21:15.940
and we've connected a lot this year,

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:16.773
which is interesting

00:21:16.773 --> 00:21:18.220
'cause we had that conversation

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.720
on Ear Biscuits about what
to do in his last year.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:22.890
And like, these two trips have like,

00:21:22.890 --> 00:21:24.570
our friendship has really blossomed.

00:21:24.570 --> 00:21:27.320
That's cool.
And we had this conversation

00:21:27.320 --> 00:21:28.380
I was telling him about this.

00:21:28.380 --> 00:21:29.310
I was like, man, we're having,

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:30.510
we're in this place,

00:21:30.510 --> 00:21:32.220
we're doing this amazing thing,

00:21:32.220 --> 00:21:34.010
and I'm kind of thinking about what

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:34.850
else we could be seeing,

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:35.830
and what am I missing?

00:21:35.830 --> 00:21:36.760
And I had to, like,

00:21:36.760 --> 00:21:38.760
I made a decision on
that first day in Maine

00:21:38.760 --> 00:21:40.510
to sort of let go of that and be like,

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:41.590
whatever I'm experiencing

00:21:41.590 --> 00:21:43.080
is what I'm supposed to be experiencing,

00:21:43.080 --> 00:21:46.370
and so I didn't have that
FOMO the rest of the trip.

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:47.250
That's good.

00:21:47.250 --> 00:21:48.170
That's very good, Rhett.

00:21:48.170 --> 00:21:51.560
But we needed to get to Yale.

00:21:51.560 --> 00:21:52.630
Now I wanna be clear,

00:21:52.630 --> 00:21:55.140
Locke is, he may be interested in Yale,

00:21:55.140 --> 00:21:57.111
but he's not Yale material.

00:21:57.111 --> 00:21:59.340
So, we're,

00:21:59.340 --> 00:22:01.020
he's not going to Yale,

00:22:01.020 --> 00:22:04.620
but one of his really
good friends is, okay?

00:22:04.620 --> 00:22:08.800
And so he, we wanted to see him.

00:22:08.800 --> 00:22:11.190
We wanted to stop and see his buddy, Ray,

00:22:11.190 --> 00:22:13.770
who we've known for years.

00:22:13.770 --> 00:22:15.220
And we were like, Ray's at Yale.

00:22:15.220 --> 00:22:16.730
I've always wanted to see Yale.

00:22:16.730 --> 00:22:18.540
Locke really wants to see Yale

00:22:18.540 --> 00:22:20.700
to see what he's not
ever going to experience.

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:22.620
Uh huh.
And you know,

00:22:22.620 --> 00:22:24.623
just to see like an Ivy League school.

00:22:26.790 --> 00:22:28.840
And I, you know, I always,

00:22:28.840 --> 00:22:31.340
I had this visions in my mind

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:35.680
of what a Northeast Ivy
League school looks like.

00:22:35.680 --> 00:22:36.820
I've never been to Harvard.

00:22:36.820 --> 00:22:38.020
I've never been to Yale.

00:22:39.155 --> 00:22:41.143
I had been texting with Ray.

00:22:43.870 --> 00:22:45.170
We were on a little group thread

00:22:45.170 --> 00:22:47.316
and I was planning the trip,

00:22:47.316 --> 00:22:48.360
and I was like, "Hey, what's your schedule

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.400
on so and so date that
we're coming through?"

00:22:51.400 --> 00:22:53.910
And he was a little slow to respond,

00:22:53.910 --> 00:22:55.601
and then we kind of worked it out.

00:22:55.601 --> 00:22:56.730
He was like, "Oh, I've got class that day,

00:22:56.730 --> 00:23:00.443
but I'll be able to like get
lunch with you guys," you know?

00:23:00.443 --> 00:23:01.360
And I was like, "Okay, well I'll plan it

00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.967
so that we're coming
through around lunchtime."

00:23:03.967 --> 00:23:05.670
"Locke and I'll probably
walk around the campus,

00:23:05.670 --> 00:23:06.503
check it out,

00:23:06.503 --> 00:23:07.900
and then we'll get lunch."

00:23:07.900 --> 00:23:09.740
We'll have lattes.
And, well,

00:23:09.740 --> 00:23:11.140
the latte already happened.

00:23:11.140 --> 00:23:14.210
And,

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:17.520
so we arrive in New Haven, Connecticut,

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:18.560
which is where Yale was,

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:20.843
which is not something
that I could have told you.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.450
And we park next to campus,

00:23:25.450 --> 00:23:26.283
and I text Ray,

00:23:26.283 --> 00:23:27.467
and I'm like, "Hey, we're here."

00:23:27.467 --> 00:23:29.297
"We're gonna walk around a little bit."

00:23:29.297 --> 00:23:30.697
"Let us know when you're outta class."

00:23:30.697 --> 00:23:31.580
"I'll come get you

00:23:31.580 --> 00:23:33.350
and we'll go to this
place that we're gonna eat

00:23:33.350 --> 00:23:34.527
that I had chosen."

00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:36.880
Consulted with him, but chosen.

00:23:36.880 --> 00:23:39.160
Yeah.
For reasons I'll get into.

00:23:39.160 --> 00:23:41.667
Ray was like, "What, you're here?"

00:23:42.577 --> 00:23:47.577
"Your text said you would
be here on so and so date

00:23:47.700 --> 00:23:49.770
a month later."

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:51.150
A month later?
So I was there

00:23:51.150 --> 00:23:52.967
on like March 20 something,

00:23:52.967 --> 00:23:54.950
and my text had said I was gonna be there

00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:55.783
on April 20 something,

00:23:55.783 --> 00:23:57.110
because when I looked at the calendar,

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:58.328
I got it wrong.

00:23:58.328 --> 00:23:59.260
By a month.

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:00.410
By a whole month.

00:24:00.410 --> 00:24:01.243
Oh wow.

00:24:01.243 --> 00:24:03.920
So was he even, was he able to meet you?

00:24:03.920 --> 00:24:04.753
First of all,

00:24:04.753 --> 00:24:06.310
let me just tell you that

00:24:06.310 --> 00:24:10.990
these circumstances bring
high amounts of shame to me.

00:24:10.990 --> 00:24:13.450
I do not like to be the
guy that makes the mistake.

00:24:13.450 --> 00:24:16.380
I hate making mistakes.

00:24:16.380 --> 00:24:17.840
You don't know your months, dude.

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:20.100
And I just beat myself up,

00:24:20.100 --> 00:24:21.470
beat myself up over it.

00:24:21.470 --> 00:24:23.170
So I was like, dang it!

00:24:23.170 --> 00:24:24.003
What the hell?

00:24:24.003 --> 00:24:24.930
What is wrong with me?

00:24:24.930 --> 00:24:27.430
Like, how did I screw this up?

00:24:27.430 --> 00:24:30.050
And, but then he immediately is like,

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:31.560
but we were there on a Monday,

00:24:31.560 --> 00:24:33.300
which was the same day
that we had planned on.

00:24:33.300 --> 00:24:34.920
And so he is like, "You know what,

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:36.457
what a welcome surprise."

00:24:36.457 --> 00:24:39.137
"My schedule's the same
as it was gonna be."

00:24:39.137 --> 00:24:40.890
"I'll text you when I'm outta class."

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:41.723
Totally fine.

00:24:41.723 --> 00:24:43.003
Huh.
So I felt great.

00:24:45.850 --> 00:24:48.900
So we meet Ray and it turns out that.

00:24:48.900 --> 00:24:50.120
He said, what a welcome surprise?

00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:51.828
That's something a Yale student would say.

00:24:51.828 --> 00:24:52.661
Yeah, since he's been at Yale,

00:24:52.661 --> 00:24:54.640
he's using terms like that, right?

00:24:54.640 --> 00:24:55.939
Yeah.

00:24:55.939 --> 00:24:59.143
I think he also used the
term sparingly one time,

00:25:00.123 --> 00:25:02.210
but he didn't use it sparingly.

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:04.483
Yeah, well, one time is pretty sparingly.

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:07.260
No, he's always been a well-spoken guy

00:25:07.260 --> 00:25:08.840
that's why he's at Yale.

00:25:08.840 --> 00:25:10.323
So he,

00:25:11.790 --> 00:25:15.610
I had been told by the guy
that I knew from Maine,

00:25:15.610 --> 00:25:17.030
but also from a little research,

00:25:17.030 --> 00:25:21.760
that New Haven, Connecticut
is known best for its pizza.

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:22.593
In fact,

00:25:22.593 --> 00:25:25.360
considered to have what
might be the best pizza

00:25:25.360 --> 00:25:26.750
in the nation,

00:25:26.750 --> 00:25:29.163
and specifically.
Is at a different style?

00:25:30.870 --> 00:25:32.370
How do you say that?

00:25:32.370 --> 00:25:33.740
Neapolitan.

00:25:33.740 --> 00:25:35.370
Yeah, I'd say it that way.

00:25:35.370 --> 00:25:36.203
It's that style.

00:25:36.203 --> 00:25:37.720
So a pizza about that big

00:25:37.720 --> 00:25:40.350
that they kind of put into a,
you know, a wood fire oven.

00:25:40.350 --> 00:25:44.043
And apparently this place, Frank Pepe's.

00:25:44.043 --> 00:25:45.135
Pepe's?

00:25:45.135 --> 00:25:46.580
Pepe's.
Pepe's, Neapolitan.

00:25:46.580 --> 00:25:49.990
Frank Pepe's is supposed to it be great.

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:52.390
In fact has been voted
the number one pizza place

00:25:52.390 --> 00:25:55.050
in the nation by some publications.

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:56.040
Oh.

00:25:56.040 --> 00:25:57.410
So I was very excited about this.

00:25:57.410 --> 00:25:59.260
Were they expecting you next month?

00:26:00.454 --> 00:26:02.400
No, I didn't make a
reservation thankfully,

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.400
'cause I would've been a month off.

00:26:04.400 --> 00:26:06.350
And it's a little bit of
like a hole in the wall

00:26:06.350 --> 00:26:09.800
kind of outside of the
main part of town situation

00:26:09.800 --> 00:26:10.780
and there's another place

00:26:10.780 --> 00:26:12.170
that's supposed to be its competitor

00:26:12.170 --> 00:26:15.760
that I didn't seem like it was
as good based on what I saw.

00:26:15.760 --> 00:26:17.080
Anyway, had a good meal.

00:26:17.080 --> 00:26:19.170
I wouldn't say it's the
best pizza I've ever had.

00:26:19.170 --> 00:26:20.003
I'm sorry, New Haven.

00:26:20.003 --> 00:26:22.270
It was very, very good.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:24.760
I did get a T-shirt, okay?

00:26:24.760 --> 00:26:26.560
It was good enough to get the T-shirt,

00:26:26.560 --> 00:26:29.010
but once you get to a
certain level of pizza,

00:26:29.010 --> 00:26:31.580
it's sort of like, how
do you go any further?

00:26:31.580 --> 00:26:32.413
You know what I mean?

00:26:32.413 --> 00:26:33.600
Okay.

00:26:33.600 --> 00:26:34.470
I get that.

00:26:34.470 --> 00:26:37.080
But interesting thing
that happened at Yale,

00:26:37.080 --> 00:26:41.260
so as I explained to you a second ago,

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:44.323
was getting recognized a fair amount.

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:49.210
Got recognized at the Paul
Revere house in Boston, right?

00:26:49.210 --> 00:26:50.395
Like, okay.

00:26:50.395 --> 00:26:53.430
I'm like, okay, this is
coming down the East Coast,

00:26:53.430 --> 00:26:55.820
there's people in the Northeast
who are Mythical Beasts.

00:26:55.820 --> 00:26:57.133
It makes me feel good.

00:26:58.150 --> 00:26:59.740
But when we get to Yale,

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:01.990
we get outta the car and we start walking

00:27:01.990 --> 00:27:03.460
and the students are getting outta class

00:27:03.460 --> 00:27:04.750
and there's just hundreds of students.

00:27:04.750 --> 00:27:06.927
And I turn to Locke and I say,

00:27:06.927 --> 00:27:08.420
"Now Locke,

00:27:08.420 --> 00:27:11.090
I don't think I'm gonna
get recognized at Yale."

00:27:11.090 --> 00:27:11.923
I was like,

00:27:11.923 --> 00:27:15.360
"These students are too
busy and too smart for us."

00:27:15.360 --> 00:27:16.757
I mean, I'm just gonna be honest with you.

00:27:16.757 --> 00:27:19.323
Oh, you're throwing the
Mythical Beasts under the bus.

00:27:19.323 --> 00:27:20.156
I'm not.

00:27:20.156 --> 00:27:22.740
Listen, I just think that if
you're at this institution,

00:27:22.740 --> 00:27:24.620
you just studying a whole lot

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.350
and you're thinking about
things on such a high level

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:27.650
that I'm sorry to say,

00:27:27.650 --> 00:27:30.180
I just don't consider
what we bring to the table

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:32.800
in terms of entertainment
to be high brow, Link.

00:27:32.800 --> 00:27:35.520
I mean, at times we may sound smart,

00:27:35.520 --> 00:27:37.120
but it's a charade.

00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:38.693
No.
And I think that,

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.340
I'm not saying that the
Mythical Beasts are stupid

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:43.173
by any means.

00:27:43.173 --> 00:27:44.420
I'm just saying that,

00:27:44.420 --> 00:27:45.520
you know, when we're getting to like

00:27:45.520 --> 00:27:50.050
the 0.1% smartest people on the planet,

00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:51.200
I just think at that point

00:27:51.200 --> 00:27:52.890
they're just not watching
a lot of YouTube videos.

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:54.730
It's my guess, right?

00:27:54.730 --> 00:27:55.563
Hmm, okay.

00:27:55.563 --> 00:27:56.513
That was my theory.

00:27:57.540 --> 00:27:59.210
My theory proved, correct.

00:27:59.210 --> 00:28:02.570
I walked past hundreds of students

00:28:02.570 --> 00:28:04.780
pouring out of classrooms.

00:28:04.780 --> 00:28:06.800
Nobody says a thing.

00:28:06.800 --> 00:28:09.830
I felt good that I had been
right in my prediction.

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:12.096
A little bit bad that I had
been right in my prediction.

00:28:12.096 --> 00:28:13.850
So.

00:28:13.850 --> 00:28:15.540
These people are, they're preoccupied.

00:28:15.540 --> 00:28:16.700
They're preoccupied.

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:17.790
They don't have time for entertainment.

00:28:17.790 --> 00:28:19.050
But,

00:28:19.050 --> 00:28:20.310
so we spend the, again,

00:28:20.310 --> 00:28:21.910
a couple of hours between,

00:28:21.910 --> 00:28:23.240
we walked around,

00:28:23.240 --> 00:28:26.110
like the way that they do it
is they live in these colleges.

00:28:26.110 --> 00:28:27.993
There's multiple colleges on a campus.

00:28:27.993 --> 00:28:30.011
It's very much designed in the way,

00:28:30.011 --> 00:28:32.390
like when I went to Oxford a while back

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:34.434
when I was in London.
Yeah.

00:28:34.434 --> 00:28:35.810
When I was in England it's,

00:28:35.810 --> 00:28:36.643
you could totally tell,

00:28:36.643 --> 00:28:38.060
they were like, we did
it this way over here

00:28:38.060 --> 00:28:39.050
and now we're gonna come over here

00:28:39.050 --> 00:28:41.720
and do these Ivy League schools
in exactly the same way.

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:43.640
So we like walked through the iron doors.

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:44.587
I mean, first of all,

00:28:44.587 --> 00:28:46.320
the campus is just,

00:28:46.320 --> 00:28:48.420
you you've been on like
Duke's campus before,

00:28:48.420 --> 00:28:50.222
which has this old school feel.

00:28:50.222 --> 00:28:52.830
Yeah.
It's like that on steroids.

00:28:52.830 --> 00:28:53.730
And the colleges,

00:28:53.730 --> 00:28:56.090
you're like, this is where
you live in this little,

00:28:56.090 --> 00:28:57.450
you have this little courtyard,

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:59.100
and you walk through this iron gate,

00:28:59.100 --> 00:29:00.450
and there's.
Medieval.

00:29:00.450 --> 00:29:01.630
Medieval stone work.

00:29:01.630 --> 00:29:03.500
Yeah, all this stone work,

00:29:03.500 --> 00:29:04.560
incredible stone work,

00:29:04.560 --> 00:29:07.910
and then you like passes by the
skull and bones like, house.

00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:09.230
Oh really?
This is where they,

00:29:09.230 --> 00:29:11.230
this is their meeting house or whatever.

00:29:12.090 --> 00:29:13.790
But again, no one's recognized me.

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:17.020
And we finish up, we had lunch with Ray.

00:29:17.020 --> 00:29:19.270
We say our goodbyes to Ray

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:21.173
and then we start walking to the car,

00:29:22.350 --> 00:29:25.840
and we're we are.
Then a whole group

00:29:25.840 --> 00:29:27.467
of Yale students says, "Surprise!"

00:29:27.467 --> 00:29:29.300
"We did know who you were!"

00:29:29.300 --> 00:29:31.410
50 Yards from the car

00:29:32.540 --> 00:29:34.857
Locke turns to me, he's like,

00:29:34.857 --> 00:29:37.560
"Dad, I actually didn't believe you

00:29:37.560 --> 00:29:38.917
but you were right."

00:29:38.917 --> 00:29:41.310
"No one at Yale recognized you."

00:29:41.310 --> 00:29:42.700
The moment,

00:29:42.700 --> 00:29:46.783
the moment he finishes
the sentence I hear,

00:29:48.512 --> 00:29:49.345
"Rhett!"

00:29:49.345 --> 00:29:50.178
"Rhett from Rhett and Link!"

00:29:50.178 --> 00:29:51.011
"Holy!"

00:29:51.011 --> 00:29:53.080
"Holy!"

00:29:53.080 --> 00:29:54.430
Oh, wow.

00:29:54.430 --> 00:29:56.743
That's a very sophisticated response.

00:29:57.700 --> 00:30:02.010
And there was a guy who
I think he recognized me.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.876
A little, a little bit.

00:30:03.876 --> 00:30:07.537
And continued to just
be like, "Man, holy!"

00:30:07.537 --> 00:30:09.340
"Rhett from Rhett and Link!"

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:13.680
So it was just like, the enthusiasm.

00:30:13.680 --> 00:30:15.310
Made up for the?

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:16.143
You know,

00:30:16.143 --> 00:30:18.400
I could get recognized by no one at Yale

00:30:18.400 --> 00:30:20.280
and to have one guy say, "Holy,"

00:30:20.280 --> 00:30:21.810
when he recognizes me.

00:30:21.810 --> 00:30:24.040
Right.
Passionately.

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:26.130
It just, it changed my whole view of Yale.

00:30:26.130 --> 00:30:28.280
It changed my whole view
of the Ivy League system.

00:30:28.280 --> 00:30:29.160
There's hope.

00:30:29.160 --> 00:30:29.993
Yeah, there's hope.

00:30:29.993 --> 00:30:30.826
For us.

00:30:31.866 --> 00:30:34.750
I will continue telling you the story.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:35.630
There's a lot more cities.

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:38.110
There's a lot more stories on the way.

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:40.670
But we do wanna talk about
"Inside Eats With Rhett &amp; Link,"

00:30:40.670 --> 00:30:41.710
our television show.

00:30:41.710 --> 00:30:43.990
I mean, it's been a while
since we had a television show.

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:44.823
It has.

00:30:44.823 --> 00:30:46.010
We do this about every 10 years.

00:30:46.010 --> 00:30:48.910
So yeah, we go behind the scenes.

00:30:48.910 --> 00:30:50.740
We get unfettered access.

00:30:50.740 --> 00:30:51.950
Well, we're kind of fettered.

00:30:51.950 --> 00:30:53.770
There's people there who are watching us,

00:30:53.770 --> 00:30:55.680
but we go to like test kitchens.

00:30:55.680 --> 00:30:59.160
We go to headquarters of
your favorite eateries,

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:00.920
food places.

00:31:00.920 --> 00:31:01.933
So I mean, Chipotle,

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:04.570
Cheesecake Factory,

00:31:04.570 --> 00:31:09.570
we also went to Cool House,
an amazing ice cream shop.

00:31:10.860 --> 00:31:12.960
That's, I'll call it syndicated.

00:31:12.960 --> 00:31:13.950
Beyond Meat.

00:31:13.950 --> 00:31:16.110
We were the first people
to ever get access

00:31:16.110 --> 00:31:18.890
to film behind the scenes at Beyond Meat,

00:31:18.890 --> 00:31:20.240
which is really cool.

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:22.480
So we learned a lot

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:25.860
and had a lot of fun.

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:28.400
So it's this interesting mix of comedy

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:31.130
and food experience,

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:32.350
and access,

00:31:32.350 --> 00:31:34.570
that creates "Inside
Eats With Rhett &amp; Link"

00:31:34.570 --> 00:31:37.290
10:30 PM. Food Network.

00:31:37.290 --> 00:31:38.123
Same time.

00:31:38.123 --> 00:31:39.733
It's launched on Discovery Plus.

00:31:40.930 --> 00:31:45.270
The extended versions
are on Discovery Plus.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:46.390
Yeah.

00:31:46.390 --> 00:31:47.560
So check it out.

00:31:47.560 --> 00:31:50.510
We've been saying things
like bonus footage,

00:31:50.510 --> 00:31:51.784
but that makes you think

00:31:51.784 --> 00:31:52.617
that you're gonna have to like watch,

00:31:52.617 --> 00:31:53.900
click on something
else, and it's like, no.

00:31:53.900 --> 00:31:55.370
The episodes are longer

00:31:55.370 --> 00:31:57.158
and have things that we had to cut out.

00:31:57.158 --> 00:31:58.040
Extended versions.
To get to the 22.

00:31:58.040 --> 00:32:00.455
Extended versions is the right word.

00:32:00.455 --> 00:32:01.288
Yeah.

00:32:01.288 --> 00:32:02.121
So, check it out.

00:32:03.190 --> 00:32:04.650
We're on Food Network.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:05.483
We're doing it.

00:32:06.843 --> 00:32:09.130
And I think, and we're just being us, man.

00:32:09.130 --> 00:32:09.963
Just being us, man.

00:32:09.963 --> 00:32:10.796
Eating stuff,

00:32:10.796 --> 00:32:12.450
having a great time.

00:32:12.450 --> 00:32:13.283
Check it out.

00:32:13.283 --> 00:32:14.260
"Inside Eats."

00:32:14.260 --> 00:32:16.760
Okay, so left New Haven.

00:32:16.760 --> 00:32:18.200
The plan is to head to New York

00:32:18.200 --> 00:32:20.140
and look at a school in New York

00:32:20.140 --> 00:32:21.460
that Locke is interested in.
Oh, wow.

00:32:21.460 --> 00:32:22.360
Yeah, 'cause that,

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:26.230
I was picturing Rhode
Island and Connecticut,

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.150
south of New York state.

00:32:28.150 --> 00:32:29.570
Nope.
I dunno why.

00:32:29.570 --> 00:32:32.490
It is funny how the geography sort of.

00:32:32.490 --> 00:32:34.380
Gets jumbled up.
Coalesces in your mind

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:35.970
as you're driving through it.

00:32:35.970 --> 00:32:37.170
The other thing that you realize

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:40.833
is that unlike any place
that I've ever lived,

00:32:43.298 --> 00:32:48.298
the concentration of population
in major cities is so high,

00:32:48.800 --> 00:32:52.090
so much higher in the
Northeast than anywhere else.

00:32:52.090 --> 00:32:54.360
Like, once you get to like North Carolina,

00:32:54.360 --> 00:32:55.540
you're gonna be driving a while

00:32:55.540 --> 00:32:57.560
before you get to a
major metropolitan area.

00:32:57.560 --> 00:32:58.470
But when you're up there,

00:32:58.470 --> 00:33:02.023
you're gonna go from like Portland,

00:33:03.924 --> 00:33:05.220
to Boston,

00:33:05.220 --> 00:33:06.330
to Providence,

00:33:06.330 --> 00:33:09.360
to New Haven, to New York, to Philly,

00:33:09.360 --> 00:33:10.840
to Baltimore,

00:33:10.840 --> 00:33:11.996
to DC.

00:33:11.996 --> 00:33:12.829
Like, there's just a bunch of big cities

00:33:12.829 --> 00:33:14.270
right there next to each other,

00:33:14.270 --> 00:33:15.950
because it's where it
all started, you know?

00:33:15.950 --> 00:33:17.810
How long were you in New York City?

00:33:17.810 --> 00:33:20.100
So we get to New York in the afternoon

00:33:20.100 --> 00:33:21.450
and you know, we're the McLaughlins,

00:33:21.450 --> 00:33:23.823
so we did not plan any official tours.

00:33:24.750 --> 00:33:25.583
Let me just say you.

00:33:25.583 --> 00:33:27.910
You saw him in New York
City, not New York state?

00:33:27.910 --> 00:33:28.950
New York City.

00:33:28.950 --> 00:33:31.950
You should plan official college tours.

00:33:31.950 --> 00:33:33.350
I'll explain it in a second.

00:33:34.620 --> 00:33:35.453
If you're gonna do,

00:33:35.453 --> 00:33:36.727
if you're gonna,

00:33:36.727 --> 00:33:38.620
because with this campus in particular,

00:33:38.620 --> 00:33:40.940
it's a pretty secure campus.

00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:43.090
Hmm.
And when we tried to

00:33:43.090 --> 00:33:43.940
just walk into it,

00:33:43.940 --> 00:33:45.680
we were stopped by security guard.

00:33:45.680 --> 00:33:47.440
He was like, "You can't
just walk in here."

00:33:47.440 --> 00:33:48.578
I was like,

00:33:48.578 --> 00:33:49.411
"Well, he's been accepted here."

00:33:49.411 --> 00:33:50.290
And he is just like, "No, I mean,

00:33:50.290 --> 00:33:51.626
no, you can't do that."

00:33:51.626 --> 00:33:53.170
"It has to be like an official thing."

00:33:53.170 --> 00:33:54.840
Hmm.
And I was like, "Okay."

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:58.900
So we ended up traveling into Manhattan.

00:33:58.900 --> 00:34:01.400
Scaling a wall?
Traveling into Manhattan

00:34:01.400 --> 00:34:02.550
and spending the night

00:34:02.550 --> 00:34:05.010
and then setting up a
tour for the next day

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:06.550
to drive back out of the city,

00:34:06.550 --> 00:34:09.210
to go to this school for an official tour.

00:34:09.210 --> 00:34:11.350
So that was a little bit of a
hiccup in our travel schedule,

00:34:11.350 --> 00:34:12.790
but it didn't really matter.

00:34:12.790 --> 00:34:13.623
So that,

00:34:13.623 --> 00:34:14.500
I'll tell you about that in a second,

00:34:14.500 --> 00:34:19.130
but that night we go to
lower East side of New York,

00:34:19.130 --> 00:34:20.730
which just happens to be
where we end up staying

00:34:20.730 --> 00:34:22.000
like every time we go to New York.

00:34:22.000 --> 00:34:22.833
Yeah.

00:34:22.833 --> 00:34:23.740
Which is a great spot,

00:34:23.740 --> 00:34:26.430
really high concentration of
great restaurants and stuff.

00:34:26.430 --> 00:34:28.430
When you have an 18 year old with you,

00:34:28.430 --> 00:34:29.850
you can't go into bars

00:34:29.850 --> 00:34:31.340
and you can't like go into comedy clubs.

00:34:31.340 --> 00:34:33.234
There's a lot of things that you can't do

00:34:33.234 --> 00:34:34.067
when you have somebody under 21.

00:34:34.067 --> 00:34:35.920
It's like, oh, this is interesting.

00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:37.460
I haven't thought about this.

00:34:37.460 --> 00:34:39.070
But what you can do is go to restaurants.

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:41.973
So we like did the whole
food tourist type thing,

00:34:41.973 --> 00:34:44.020
when you go to a restaurant

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:45.790
and you just get a couple
of things and you leave

00:34:45.790 --> 00:34:46.690
and you go to another restaurant

00:34:46.690 --> 00:34:47.640
and get a couple of things.

00:34:47.640 --> 00:34:49.040
I just, I,

00:34:49.040 --> 00:34:51.130
this style of vacation

00:34:51.130 --> 00:34:53.690
has really like opened
a whole world to me,

00:34:53.690 --> 00:34:55.270
this road trip vacation,

00:34:55.270 --> 00:34:56.840
where you just drop into a city

00:34:56.840 --> 00:35:00.200
and eat a couple of things at
a few different restaurants.

00:35:00.200 --> 00:35:02.190
It might be my new way of vacationing.

00:35:02.190 --> 00:35:04.710
It's just, I don't necessarily mean

00:35:05.650 --> 00:35:08.200
a full top of the nation

00:35:08.200 --> 00:35:09.610
to the bottom of the nation road trip,

00:35:09.610 --> 00:35:10.443
but it might be like,

00:35:10.443 --> 00:35:11.650
I'm gonna land in one state,

00:35:11.650 --> 00:35:13.690
spend a couple of days in this place

00:35:13.690 --> 00:35:15.070
and I'm gonna go to this other state

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:15.903
and this other city.

00:35:15.903 --> 00:35:17.850
Like, this dual city thing.

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:20.504
So I'm definitely floating
this idea with Jesse.

00:35:20.504 --> 00:35:22.080
Well you're getting ready for Europe then.

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:23.478
That's how you can do Europe.

00:35:23.478 --> 00:35:24.811
Yeah, exactly.

00:35:25.730 --> 00:35:27.620
So, we spend some time in Manhattan,

00:35:27.620 --> 00:35:28.919
have some great food,

00:35:28.919 --> 00:35:29.970
then we go back, we do the official tour,

00:35:29.970 --> 00:35:31.090
which was illuminating,

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:32.360
'cause we'd done some unofficial tours.

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:35.770
But like when you can like get
into the buildings and stuff,

00:35:35.770 --> 00:35:36.780
and like,

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:38.890
you don't have to like, wait
till a student opens a door

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:40.280
and then run in behind them.

00:35:40.280 --> 00:35:42.010
It's much better.

00:35:42.010 --> 00:35:43.480
And then you also get to ask questions

00:35:43.480 --> 00:35:45.390
to people who have
experience at this college.

00:35:45.390 --> 00:35:46.440
Okay, yeah.

00:35:46.440 --> 00:35:48.853
So I felt, I was like, oh man, this is.

00:35:50.960 --> 00:35:53.050
Lesson learned for Shepherd
is what I kept telling Locke.

00:35:53.050 --> 00:35:55.590
I was like, okay, well
me and Shepherd do this.

00:35:55.590 --> 00:35:57.947
I'm gonna do it better
than I do it with you.

00:35:57.947 --> 00:35:58.780
Hmm.
Okay.

00:35:58.780 --> 00:35:59.613
And you don't have to,

00:35:59.613 --> 00:36:00.606
you don't have to do what I did.

00:36:00.606 --> 00:36:01.570
If you have a kid, do official tours.

00:36:01.570 --> 00:36:03.020
You're probably already thinking that.

00:36:03.020 --> 00:36:05.580
Now, I knew I wanted to
drive to Philadelphia,

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:06.720
and again,

00:36:06.720 --> 00:36:09.110
we were gonna go to Pat's and Geno's

00:36:09.110 --> 00:36:11.150
and eat a cheese steak from both places.

00:36:11.150 --> 00:36:11.983
Oh, both places.

00:36:11.983 --> 00:36:15.040
So, the thing was to
take Locke to this place

00:36:16.040 --> 00:36:17.570
where I've got this experience

00:36:17.570 --> 00:36:19.750
from making this video in 2008.

00:36:19.750 --> 00:36:21.210
Yeah.

00:36:21.210 --> 00:36:23.930
On the Alka-Seltzer
Great American Road Trip.

00:36:23.930 --> 00:36:24.763
And I was like,

00:36:24.763 --> 00:36:26.860
we're gonna taste these cheese steaks

00:36:26.860 --> 00:36:28.560
and then you're gonna help me determine

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:29.393
which one's the best,

00:36:29.393 --> 00:36:31.080
and then we're gonna get
the T-shirt from the place

00:36:31.080 --> 00:36:32.650
that we think has got
the best cheese steak.

00:36:32.650 --> 00:36:33.960
But,

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:35.850
I hear a but coming.

00:36:35.850 --> 00:36:38.423
So we go to Geno's first, okay?

00:36:39.710 --> 00:36:40.543
The flashy one.

00:36:40.543 --> 00:36:42.570
Flashy, orange color,

00:36:42.570 --> 00:36:44.750
you know, he's the one
that's the big personality,

00:36:44.750 --> 00:36:48.100
a bit of a controversial
character as well.

00:36:48.100 --> 00:36:50.627
I think he had a sign
on the door for years.

00:36:50.627 --> 00:36:52.270
It was just like, if you
can't order in English,

00:36:52.270 --> 00:36:54.453
don't order at all or something like that.

00:36:55.960 --> 00:36:57.800
I think it's since been taken down,

00:36:57.800 --> 00:36:58.750
but I was like,

00:36:58.750 --> 00:37:01.170
okay, but these are the
classic cheese steak places.

00:37:01.170 --> 00:37:02.700
We're gonna get one from both of them.

00:37:02.700 --> 00:37:04.240
We get up to Geno's

00:37:04.240 --> 00:37:05.450
and immediately

00:37:05.450 --> 00:37:07.930
the guys who are cooking
the steaks back there

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:12.220
who are not old enough to
have worked there since 2008,

00:37:12.220 --> 00:37:13.840
these guys are in their twenties.

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:17.477
Yeah.
Are like, "Rhett!"

00:37:17.477 --> 00:37:18.870
"Rhett from the video.

00:37:18.870 --> 00:37:19.703
From the video?

00:37:19.703 --> 00:37:20.536
Yeah.
Oh, wow.

00:37:20.536 --> 00:37:21.410
They all see the video.

00:37:21.410 --> 00:37:23.680
So it's not like Rhett
from Good Mythical Morning.

00:37:23.680 --> 00:37:26.010
It's just like this video

00:37:26.010 --> 00:37:29.680
apparently is something that
if you work at Pat's and Geno's

00:37:29.680 --> 00:37:31.420
you've seen this video.

00:37:31.420 --> 00:37:32.980
And so then they were
like, he knows what's up.

00:37:32.980 --> 00:37:34.421
He came back to the right place.

00:37:34.421 --> 00:37:35.254
And I wasn't about to say,

00:37:35.254 --> 00:37:36.657
I'm going to Pat's right after this.

00:37:36.657 --> 00:37:39.160
So we get one cheese steak.

00:37:39.160 --> 00:37:39.993
Now, first of all,

00:37:39.993 --> 00:37:42.070
Locke's a little bit picky, okay?

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:43.730
And I kind of knew

00:37:43.730 --> 00:37:46.120
this was gonna be a little
bit difficult for him

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:48.340
because I was like the way
that they suggest you order it

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:49.320
is with Cheez Whiz,

00:37:49.320 --> 00:37:50.153
and he was like, "Dad,

00:37:50.153 --> 00:37:52.420
I can't eat something
with Chez Wiz on it."

00:37:52.420 --> 00:37:54.190
Like, he just won't do it.

00:37:54.190 --> 00:37:55.870
And so I was like, all right,
well you're probably right.

00:37:55.870 --> 00:37:56.970
Let's get the one with provolone.

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:58.047
It was just better anyway, right?

00:37:58.047 --> 00:37:59.370
That's what I thought.

00:37:59.370 --> 00:38:00.373
So we get like,

00:38:01.250 --> 00:38:02.920
you know, there's a special
way you have to order it.

00:38:02.920 --> 00:38:04.730
They're not as strict at
Geno's as they're at Pat's

00:38:04.730 --> 00:38:07.363
about saying it all in the
right order or whatever.

00:38:08.790 --> 00:38:12.540
But we get it, and we
split it, and we eat it.

00:38:12.540 --> 00:38:16.160
And first thing I'm
struggling with is like,

00:38:16.160 --> 00:38:17.813
this is not that great.

00:38:18.700 --> 00:38:19.920
Just gonna be honest with you.

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:21.190
I'm like, I've had a lot
of great cheese steaks

00:38:21.190 --> 00:38:22.240
and I've had a lot of good sandwiches,

00:38:22.240 --> 00:38:23.714
and I was just like.
Yeah.

00:38:23.714 --> 00:38:25.993
This is just not that great,

00:38:27.710 --> 00:38:29.500
but it's pretty good.

00:38:29.500 --> 00:38:31.730
But it's not, if I made
a cheese steak at home,

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:33.260
if I threw something
together with some good bread

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:34.210
and some cheese steak meat,

00:38:34.210 --> 00:38:36.713
like I'd be getting pretty
close to this, right?

00:38:37.780 --> 00:38:38.613
And Locke's like,

00:38:38.613 --> 00:38:39.446
"Yeah, it's good."

00:38:39.446 --> 00:38:40.750
