there's steps that go down,

00:58:18.450 --> 00:58:19.673
and you can tell,

00:58:19.673 --> 00:58:21.130
obviously tell you're entering into

00:58:21.130 --> 00:58:25.020
what feels like a Southern
Rainforest Cafe kind of vibe.

00:58:25.020 --> 00:58:26.210
Oh.
Because essentially

00:58:26.210 --> 00:58:29.590
it was this pastor who has since died,

00:58:29.590 --> 00:58:31.730
but they've kept it alive.

00:58:31.730 --> 00:58:33.230
They've kept the museum alive.

00:58:34.100 --> 00:58:36.310
It is basically a collection of taxidermy

00:58:37.330 --> 00:58:38.960
of everything you can imagine.

00:58:38.960 --> 00:58:40.180
What?

00:58:40.180 --> 00:58:41.360
And also a bunch of tools.

00:58:41.360 --> 00:58:44.517
So it's the Creation
Museum and the tool museum.

00:58:44.517 --> 00:58:46.780
And it shows up on like
roadside attraction type stuff,

00:58:46.780 --> 00:58:49.483
because it's so quirky.
Quirky.

00:58:50.450 --> 00:58:51.283
What?

00:58:51.283 --> 00:58:53.518
This are just normal animals?

00:58:53.518 --> 00:58:55.120
There's like a North Carolina section,

00:58:55.120 --> 00:58:56.860
which is like everything that you can kill

00:58:56.860 --> 00:58:58.940
in North Carolina, they're on display,

00:58:58.940 --> 00:59:03.820
but there's also just like
mostly American things.

00:59:03.820 --> 00:59:04.840
So it's just like,

00:59:04.840 --> 00:59:06.540
look at all these, God made this animal.

00:59:06.540 --> 00:59:07.800
Look, God made this animal.

00:59:07.800 --> 00:59:09.730
But then there's little exhibits.

00:59:09.730 --> 00:59:11.670
Like, pretty early on

00:59:11.670 --> 00:59:14.240
there is a cabinet that is a glass cabinet

00:59:14.240 --> 00:59:16.570
and there is nothing
inside of the cabinet.

00:59:16.570 --> 00:59:17.967
And on the top it says,

00:59:17.967 --> 00:59:19.870
"Inside this cabinet

00:59:19.870 --> 00:59:22.690
is all the credible
evidence for evolution."

00:59:22.690 --> 00:59:24.040
So it's things like that.

00:59:24.040 --> 00:59:26.570
But there was a little snake with an apple

00:59:26.570 --> 00:59:28.840
inside the cabinet, which was like Satan,

00:59:28.840 --> 00:59:30.583
the serpent inside the cabinet.

00:59:30.583 --> 00:59:31.416
Oh.

00:59:31.416 --> 00:59:33.160
Now this guy was like
serious about all this.

00:59:33.160 --> 00:59:34.790
Like, he didn't necessarily
have a sense of humor

00:59:34.790 --> 00:59:35.710
about any of this.

00:59:35.710 --> 00:59:37.280
And I didn't know how the people

00:59:37.280 --> 00:59:38.790
who currently run the Christian Bookstore

00:59:38.790 --> 00:59:39.770
were going to feel about this.

00:59:39.770 --> 00:59:41.230
'Cause I'm obviously
coming in there as a guy

00:59:41.230 --> 00:59:42.840
who's like, I disagree with everything

00:59:42.840 --> 00:59:44.560
that you're talking about.

00:59:44.560 --> 00:59:45.810
Wild, man.
But I like,

00:59:45.810 --> 00:59:47.840
I'm fascinated by this mentality

00:59:47.840 --> 00:59:49.880
and I understand it

00:59:49.880 --> 00:59:51.513
'cause I've known so many
people who believe this

00:59:51.513 --> 00:59:54.010
that like this will be a
fun thing for us to do.

00:59:54.010 --> 00:59:56.160
And I'm not going in
there to make fun of them

00:59:56.160 --> 00:59:57.360
to their face.

00:59:57.360 --> 00:59:59.090
I mean, obviously we're going
in there to be like, what?

00:59:59.090 --> 00:59:59.923
This is crazy.

00:59:59.923 --> 01:00:01.610
We're kind of laughing,

01:00:01.610 --> 01:00:03.580
but I'm not gonna laugh in their face.

01:00:03.580 --> 01:00:05.660
And I was also like,

01:00:05.660 --> 01:00:06.770
you know, how much does it cost?

01:00:06.770 --> 01:00:09.328
And they were like, "Whatever
you think it's worth."

01:00:09.328 --> 01:00:10.161
Ha.

01:00:10.161 --> 01:00:12.814
And so I did drop 20 bucks
into the donation thing.

01:00:12.814 --> 01:00:13.748
I'm like, I'm gonna come here

01:00:13.748 --> 01:00:14.581
and I'm, you know what?

01:00:14.581 --> 01:00:16.270
I'm gonna send a lot
of people to you guys,

01:00:16.270 --> 01:00:17.940
because if you are in Southern Pines,

01:00:17.940 --> 01:00:19.300
go to the Christian Bookstore,

01:00:19.300 --> 01:00:20.520
look at the Creation Museum.

01:00:20.520 --> 01:00:22.150
It's fascinating.

01:00:22.150 --> 01:00:23.700
There's a whole section.

01:00:23.700 --> 01:00:24.533
It's very,

01:00:24.533 --> 01:00:27.903
it's kind of crazy how much
crap this guy collected.

01:00:29.040 --> 01:00:30.430
He collected these tools

01:00:30.430 --> 01:00:33.450
that I didn't even ever
really figure out what it was.

01:00:33.450 --> 01:00:34.283
They looked like levels.

01:00:34.283 --> 01:00:36.160
He was really like,
he's got all these old,

01:00:36.160 --> 01:00:38.080
certain type of tool.

01:00:38.080 --> 01:00:40.270
Like, found antiques?

01:00:40.270 --> 01:00:41.640
But a whole bunch of the same thing.

01:00:41.640 --> 01:00:43.595
Like, hundreds of the same thing.

01:00:43.595 --> 01:00:45.090
And I'm like, I don't even
know what this thing is.

01:00:45.090 --> 01:00:46.283
I'm afraid to ask.

01:00:47.140 --> 01:00:48.490
You're not a placard reader.

01:00:48.490 --> 01:00:50.400
But there is a section

01:00:51.250 --> 01:00:52.820
where you'll go down a little hallway

01:00:52.820 --> 01:00:55.180
and come to this glass case,

01:00:55.180 --> 01:00:58.340
and it'll say, these are the things,

01:00:58.340 --> 01:01:01.663
these are the other gods
that the world worships.

01:01:02.780 --> 01:01:07.276
And it'll be like, like country music.

01:01:07.276 --> 01:01:08.109
What?

01:01:08.109 --> 01:01:09.740
And then it'll be like a guy

01:01:09.740 --> 01:01:11.440
on the front of a muscle magazine,

01:01:12.760 --> 01:01:14.120
or like a woman,

01:01:14.120 --> 01:01:15.460
but not a woman in a bikini,

01:01:15.460 --> 01:01:18.920
but like suggesting like
these are the other things.

01:01:18.920 --> 01:01:20.470
But then mixed in with the vices.

01:01:20.470 --> 01:01:22.320
So country music,

01:01:22.320 --> 01:01:24.440
muscles, and sexiness.

01:01:24.440 --> 01:01:26.810
But you know, the worldly pleasures.

01:01:26.810 --> 01:01:30.080
But mixed in indiscriminately
where the worldly pleasures

01:01:30.080 --> 01:01:32.990
is like something that represents

01:01:32.990 --> 01:01:34.743
like an African tribal religion.

01:01:35.694 --> 01:01:38.730
Like so like other
religions, like Buddhism.

01:01:38.730 --> 01:01:40.400
So like, yeah again.
Okay.

01:01:40.400 --> 01:01:41.530
You gotta understand,

01:01:41.530 --> 01:01:43.850
I totally understand this way of thinking.

01:01:43.850 --> 01:01:46.470
I used to kind of think
this way growing up.

01:01:46.470 --> 01:01:48.800
You thought that all these other religions

01:01:48.800 --> 01:01:52.290
were essentially like satanic
expressions of things.

01:01:52.290 --> 01:01:53.900
They weren't like,
somebody's like heritage

01:01:53.900 --> 01:01:55.800
and the same type of
thing that you had done

01:01:55.800 --> 01:01:57.150
and come up with your own deal.

01:01:57.150 --> 01:01:58.100
Yeah.
You thought that

01:01:58.100 --> 01:01:59.600
they were wrong and you were right.

01:01:59.600 --> 01:02:03.320
And so you could put them along
with all these other vices,

01:02:03.320 --> 01:02:05.440
like worrying too much about your body,

01:02:05.440 --> 01:02:06.840
or caring too about food,

01:02:06.840 --> 01:02:08.473
or caring too much about music.

01:02:09.930 --> 01:02:11.540
And the boys were fascinated.

01:02:11.540 --> 01:02:12.373
They were just like, what?

01:02:12.373 --> 01:02:13.350
I don't.

01:02:13.350 --> 01:02:15.100
They couldn't process it.

01:02:15.100 --> 01:02:16.972
They were like, I don't
understand what he's saying.

01:02:16.972 --> 01:02:17.805
I'm like, no.

01:02:17.805 --> 01:02:18.638
Well, let me understand.

01:02:18.638 --> 01:02:21.790
He's saying that everything
outside of God and Jesus,

01:02:21.790 --> 01:02:23.270
as understood

01:02:23.270 --> 01:02:25.160
in a very particular
interpretation of the Bible,

01:02:25.160 --> 01:02:27.800
everything outside of that is wrong.

01:02:27.800 --> 01:02:29.300
That's what this guy thought,

01:02:29.300 --> 01:02:31.410
and that's what a lot of
people still do think.

01:02:31.410 --> 01:02:33.150
It's the source of a lot of strife.

01:02:33.150 --> 01:02:34.207
And you gave him $20.

01:02:34.207 --> 01:02:36.090
And I gave him $20, right.

01:02:36.090 --> 01:02:37.830
So we saw the Creation Museum.

01:02:37.830 --> 01:02:38.960
Highly recommend it.

01:02:38.960 --> 01:02:42.560
And then we were on our way
to Florence, South Carolina,

01:02:42.560 --> 01:02:46.400
to stop at this hole in
the wall barbecue place

01:02:46.400 --> 01:02:48.540
that I had read about.

01:02:48.540 --> 01:02:49.900
But little did I know,

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:51.610
that kind of coming from Southern Pines

01:02:51.610 --> 01:02:53.450
and getting to Florence, South Carolina,

01:02:53.450 --> 01:02:56.820
we were gonna go right
past South Of The Border.

01:02:56.820 --> 01:02:59.370
This was an unintended stop.

01:02:59.370 --> 01:03:01.130
So if you've ever been on I-95

01:03:01.130 --> 01:03:02.900
going from North Carolina
to South Carolina.

01:03:02.900 --> 01:03:04.120
You got sucked in, huh?

01:03:04.120 --> 01:03:04.953
You see,

01:03:04.953 --> 01:03:06.240
well, you can't not stop there

01:03:06.240 --> 01:03:07.920
when you're on a road trip with your kids.

01:03:07.920 --> 01:03:08.840
Yeah.

01:03:08.840 --> 01:03:11.600
Now you see all the signs that.

01:03:11.600 --> 01:03:13.770
Every mile there's a billboard.

01:03:13.770 --> 01:03:14.902
Let me just, I'm just gonna.

01:03:14.902 --> 01:03:16.135
Stop and see Pedro.
I'll just give you an idea.

01:03:16.135 --> 01:03:17.900
Mascot.

01:03:17.900 --> 01:03:22.900
This is a, definitely

01:03:23.100 --> 01:03:28.100
highly racist stereotype of
a Mexican man named Pedro

01:03:29.700 --> 01:03:33.320
who wears a giant sombrero
and has a mustache

01:03:33.320 --> 01:03:35.230
and is welcoming you to South Carolina

01:03:35.230 --> 01:03:38.660
as if you're passing
from America to Mexico.

01:03:38.660 --> 01:03:40.010
It was somebody's grand idea

01:03:40.010 --> 01:03:41.730
many, many decades ago

01:03:41.730 --> 01:03:44.900
to like have this
Mexican-themed play land.

01:03:44.900 --> 01:03:45.870
There's a motel there.

01:03:45.870 --> 01:03:46.930
There's a putt putt place there.

01:03:46.930 --> 01:03:50.080
There's a giant Pedro with
a giant sombrero tower

01:03:50.080 --> 01:03:51.580
that you can go into.
Yeah.

01:03:52.460 --> 01:03:53.870
So we stopped there.

01:03:53.870 --> 01:03:55.310
How is it still?

01:03:55.310 --> 01:03:56.630
It's not doing well, man.

01:03:56.630 --> 01:03:57.670
I mean,

01:03:57.670 --> 01:04:00.240
we literally were inside the souvenir shop

01:04:00.240 --> 01:04:01.500
and the power went out.

01:04:01.500 --> 01:04:03.740
Like it just, the whole
place just went black.

01:04:03.740 --> 01:04:04.573
I guess they shut down while we here.

01:04:04.573 --> 01:04:05.797
You witnessed the end.

01:04:05.797 --> 01:04:07.400
We witnessed the end
of South Of The Border.

01:04:07.400 --> 01:04:08.980
Wow.
But the boys, again,

01:04:08.980 --> 01:04:10.170
you know, they're growing up in

01:04:10.170 --> 01:04:14.950
much more informed times than we did.

01:04:14.950 --> 01:04:15.783
And so like, they're just like,

01:04:15.783 --> 01:04:18.460
"Dad, this is like so racist."

01:04:18.460 --> 01:04:19.317
I'm like, "Yes, son."

01:04:19.317 --> 01:04:21.450
"I did not realize that
when I was your age,

01:04:21.450 --> 01:04:23.290
but you've been educated differently."

01:04:23.290 --> 01:04:24.820
So we decided to not get a T-shirt.

01:04:24.820 --> 01:04:26.990
I was like, I, you know?

01:04:26.990 --> 01:04:27.823
Okay, yeah.

01:04:27.823 --> 01:04:31.103
I don't really wanna play
into this whole thing,

01:04:32.080 --> 01:04:33.460
but it's still there

01:04:33.460 --> 01:04:35.540
and it's become a little
bit of an artifact.

01:04:35.540 --> 01:04:36.373
Yeah.

01:04:36.373 --> 01:04:37.850
In and of itself.

01:04:37.850 --> 01:04:40.460
But we were gonna go to
this hole in the wall,

01:04:40.460 --> 01:04:41.850
barbecue place.

01:04:41.850 --> 01:04:43.920
Now I just heard about this place.

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:48.920
I'm actually I'm torn with mentioning it

01:04:49.070 --> 01:04:51.160
because I don't, it was,

01:04:51.160 --> 01:04:53.593
the barbecue was very, very good,

01:04:54.715 --> 01:04:56.323
but the guy is like,

01:04:57.460 --> 01:04:59.520
so politically,

01:04:59.520 --> 01:05:02.400
he's really just set himself up.

01:05:02.400 --> 01:05:05.776
His barbecue put has become
like a political statement.

01:05:05.776 --> 01:05:07.310
Oh.

01:05:07.310 --> 01:05:09.331
So you can find it if you want to.

01:05:09.331 --> 01:05:10.280
You're not gonna have
to do too much research,

01:05:10.280 --> 01:05:12.530
but I won't give 'em the free advertising,

01:05:12.530 --> 01:05:15.253
but the barbecue was impeccable.

01:05:15.253 --> 01:05:16.920
Really?

01:05:16.920 --> 01:05:18.160
Mustard based?

01:05:18.160 --> 01:05:18.993
It wasn't.

01:05:18.993 --> 01:05:20.980
So mustard based is kind of isolated more

01:05:20.980 --> 01:05:22.020
to the Columbia area,

01:05:22.020 --> 01:05:23.230
and we weren't going through Columbia,

01:05:23.230 --> 01:05:24.063
and so I was,

01:05:24.063 --> 01:05:26.800
they had a mustard base
that you could put on things

01:05:26.800 --> 01:05:28.630
that we did kind of get,

01:05:28.630 --> 01:05:30.360
but the barbecue,

01:05:30.360 --> 01:05:33.100
we got pulled pork sandwiches
and they were pre-sauced,

01:05:33.100 --> 01:05:36.050
but sauce in a good way.

01:05:36.050 --> 01:05:38.530
And just, and pretty finely chopped.

01:05:38.530 --> 01:05:40.850
So very similar to North
Carolina in that there's,

01:05:40.850 --> 01:05:42.190
like, it's not pulled pork.

01:05:42.190 --> 01:05:43.720
It's chopped pork,

01:05:43.720 --> 01:05:45.830
which is not my preference
if I'm doing it myself.

01:05:45.830 --> 01:05:46.780
Yeah, me neither.

01:05:48.100 --> 01:05:51.080
I like to make whatever
I'm eating in the moment

01:05:51.080 --> 01:05:51.913
my preference,

01:05:51.913 --> 01:05:53.880
'cause I have a lot better time with it.

01:05:53.880 --> 01:05:57.160
So, and I did that and it was exquisite.

01:05:57.160 --> 01:05:58.840
It was great.

01:05:58.840 --> 01:06:03.530
It's a food truck that is
next to a dining facility,

01:06:03.530 --> 01:06:04.670
like a dining room

01:06:05.720 --> 01:06:07.330
on just this random road.

01:06:07.330 --> 01:06:08.840
Like a shelter.

01:06:08.840 --> 01:06:11.860
No, it's like, you could have
put the restaurant in here,

01:06:11.860 --> 01:06:13.250
but you didn't.

01:06:13.250 --> 01:06:14.697
'Cause you want to drive it away?

01:06:14.697 --> 01:06:16.440
But there's a pool table
in the middle of it,

01:06:16.440 --> 01:06:18.500
and Fox News was on,

01:06:18.500 --> 01:06:19.700
on the television,

01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:22.950
and then we all purposely
sit right next to

01:06:22.950 --> 01:06:25.090
'cause we were the only ones in there,

01:06:25.090 --> 01:06:28.110
a giant picture of Donald Trump.

01:06:28.110 --> 01:06:29.320
The picture was so big.

01:06:29.320 --> 01:06:31.030
What?
That when you're next to it,

01:06:31.030 --> 01:06:31.863
it was like,

01:06:31.863 --> 01:06:32.696
you couldn't even tell what;

01:06:32.696 --> 01:06:33.990
it's so pixelated.

01:06:33.990 --> 01:06:36.190
Like, you could see that they
had printed this thing out

01:06:36.190 --> 01:06:38.500
and then they did not have a
picture that was large enough.

01:06:38.500 --> 01:06:40.260
The resolution of the Trump picture

01:06:40.260 --> 01:06:42.180
wasn't large enough for this sign,

01:06:42.180 --> 01:06:43.380
but it says,

01:06:43.380 --> 01:06:45.850
it's got Republican with a check box,

01:06:45.850 --> 01:06:47.230
Democrat with a check box,

01:06:47.230 --> 01:06:49.210
and then Trump with a check box,

01:06:49.210 --> 01:06:50.540
and it's checked Trump,

01:06:50.540 --> 01:06:52.760
and hasn't checked Republican or Democrat.

01:06:52.760 --> 01:06:53.773
Wow.
So that's the statement

01:06:53.773 --> 01:06:54.870
that this guy's making.

01:06:54.870 --> 01:06:57.730
And there's a lot of other
questionable paraphernalia

01:06:57.730 --> 01:06:59.110
and some stuff on the wall
and that kind of thing.

01:06:59.110 --> 01:07:01.100
But the thing is, is that,

01:07:01.100 --> 01:07:04.940
that is what the south is
in a lot of ways, right?

01:07:04.940 --> 01:07:06.530
Like if you get outside of the cities,

01:07:06.530 --> 01:07:07.550
that's where you,

01:07:07.550 --> 01:07:09.400
that's the mentality that
you're gonna run into

01:07:09.400 --> 01:07:10.680
into a lot of the people.

01:07:10.680 --> 01:07:12.390
And I was just basically
just telling the boys,

01:07:12.390 --> 01:07:13.420
I'm like, this is,

01:07:13.420 --> 01:07:14.860
man, this is where I come from.

01:07:14.860 --> 01:07:18.060
Like, this is our background

01:07:18.060 --> 01:07:19.980
and this is the barbecue
that the they make.

01:07:19.980 --> 01:07:23.320
And I'm not gonna just
not go there, you know,

01:07:23.320 --> 01:07:26.618
because they've identified
with this racist soul,

01:07:26.618 --> 01:07:28.483
but you know,

01:07:28.483 --> 01:07:31.587
it was a little bit of an
eye opening experience,

01:07:31.587 --> 01:07:34.660
but they also kind of loved it in a sense.

01:07:34.660 --> 01:07:36.930
Not love for what that dude stands for

01:07:36.930 --> 01:07:38.670
and what the mentality that those people,

01:07:38.670 --> 01:07:41.430
but it's almost like going
into a foreign place.

01:07:41.430 --> 01:07:42.330
Yeah.

01:07:42.330 --> 01:07:44.330
Now when you're living in Los Angeles,

01:07:44.330 --> 01:07:47.160
you don't get to see that, you know?

01:07:47.160 --> 01:07:48.793
Right.
And we have the privilege

01:07:48.793 --> 01:07:52.270
as three white dudes just
rolling through the countryside.

01:07:52.270 --> 01:07:53.720
I'm not worried about my safety.

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:57.310
I might get some side eye
because of my hair or whatever,

01:07:57.310 --> 01:07:58.787
or the clothes I've got on.

01:07:58.787 --> 01:08:00.080
Yeah.
But I'm not gonna,

01:08:00.080 --> 01:08:01.710
my life is not in danger.

01:08:01.710 --> 01:08:02.640
I don't feel unsafe.

01:08:02.640 --> 01:08:04.690
I don't feel like I need to
make sure I'm in my hotel

01:08:04.690 --> 01:08:05.710
before the sun goes down.

01:08:05.710 --> 01:08:06.543
Right.

01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:09.670
Which if my skin was a different color,

01:08:09.670 --> 01:08:10.580
I might feel that way.

01:08:10.580 --> 01:08:12.410
So I recognize the privilege
of being able to like

01:08:12.410 --> 01:08:16.360
go and experience these things
in almost like a historian,

01:08:16.360 --> 01:08:17.193
like an observer,

01:08:17.193 --> 01:08:18.840
like a journalist type way,

01:08:18.840 --> 01:08:19.890
because I can just come back

01:08:19.890 --> 01:08:22.420
to my bubble over here in Los Angeles

01:08:22.420 --> 01:08:24.263
and not have to see any of this stuff.

01:08:25.410 --> 01:08:26.290
But great barbecue.

01:08:26.290 --> 01:08:27.610
If you happen to figure out where it is

01:08:27.610 --> 01:08:29.510
based on what I just said.

01:08:29.510 --> 01:08:31.630
We stopped in Savannah, Georgia.

01:08:31.630 --> 01:08:32.590
Again, now you're talking like,

01:08:32.590 --> 01:08:34.350
you see how quickly we're moving, right?

01:08:34.350 --> 01:08:35.183
Yeah.

01:08:35.183 --> 01:08:37.690
So we go from North Carolina,

01:08:37.690 --> 01:08:38.523
we're in North Carolina,

01:08:38.523 --> 01:08:39.940
we eat lunch in South Carolina,

01:08:39.940 --> 01:08:43.210
and then we're in Savannah,
Georgia for dinner.

01:08:43.210 --> 01:08:44.690
Savannah's cool.

01:08:44.690 --> 01:08:47.416
Savannah is awesome.

01:08:47.416 --> 01:08:50.690
I was actually, I know
Birmingham is really cool.

01:08:50.690 --> 01:08:51.790
Savannah is a different thing.

01:08:51.790 --> 01:08:53.610
Savannah, I don't think.
Savannah,

01:08:53.610 --> 01:08:55.390
it's on the ocean.

01:08:55.390 --> 01:08:56.223
Oh yeah.

01:08:56.223 --> 01:08:57.193
We've been, university, no.

01:08:57.193 --> 01:08:58.670
Have we been there?

01:08:58.670 --> 01:08:59.840
I've been to Savannah before,

01:08:59.840 --> 01:09:00.977
but I don't know if I was with you.

01:09:00.977 --> 01:09:01.810
I don't think I've been to Savannah.

01:09:01.810 --> 01:09:04.950
Savannah is everything that you imagine

01:09:04.950 --> 01:09:06.790
in terms of like the deep south

01:09:06.790 --> 01:09:08.940
with the mossy trees and the humidity.

01:09:08.940 --> 01:09:10.960
Again, the temperature
had changed significantly

01:09:10.960 --> 01:09:12.390
by the time we get down here to Georgia

01:09:12.390 --> 01:09:14.520
and it's basically, there's
a small part of Georgia

01:09:14.520 --> 01:09:15.970
that's on the coast,

01:09:15.970 --> 01:09:17.130
and Savannah is there.

01:09:17.130 --> 01:09:20.210
There's like Augusta at
the top and then Savannah.

01:09:20.210 --> 01:09:21.515
Okay.

01:09:21.515 --> 01:09:23.020
And Savannah is old,

01:09:23.020 --> 01:09:24.570
and beautiful,

01:09:24.570 --> 01:09:28.240
and it kind of has a
little bit of a Charleston

01:09:28.240 --> 01:09:29.610
meets New Orleans,

01:09:29.610 --> 01:09:30.443
kind of like,

01:09:30.443 --> 01:09:32.090
there's just, there's a high concentration

01:09:32.090 --> 01:09:34.880
of really great things to eat.

01:09:34.880 --> 01:09:35.990
Lots of things to do.

01:09:35.990 --> 01:09:38.540
There was like a music festival going on.

01:09:38.540 --> 01:09:40.290
There's like soul food,

01:09:40.290 --> 01:09:44.627
Southern cooking restaurants
on every single corner, right?

01:09:44.627 --> 01:09:47.240
And of course we didn't know
when we were gonna be there,

01:09:47.240 --> 01:09:48.410
so I'm not doing any reservations.

01:09:48.410 --> 01:09:49.540
I'm just like getting there,

01:09:49.540 --> 01:09:50.950
and maybe there's a place
that I've researched

01:09:50.950 --> 01:09:52.110
ahead of time.

01:09:52.110 --> 01:09:53.864
But like, we like walk into places

01:09:53.864 --> 01:09:55.040
and it was like, we don't have any tables,

01:09:55.040 --> 01:09:56.765
but we did find a place,

01:09:56.765 --> 01:09:57.598
and that's what I wanted to give you.

01:09:57.598 --> 01:09:58.431
This is what we ordered,

01:09:58.431 --> 01:10:00.250
just to give you an idea of the food.

01:10:01.990 --> 01:10:06.990
We had low country, mac and
cheese with shrimp and sausage.

01:10:07.560 --> 01:10:09.910
Oh!
That's what Shepherd got.

01:10:09.910 --> 01:10:11.773
I got gumbo and grits.

01:10:13.090 --> 01:10:16.250
Which had everything from
gumbo and cheese grits,

01:10:16.250 --> 01:10:18.193
and Locke got country fried bison.

01:10:19.120 --> 01:10:20.220
And we did just kind of shared it all.

01:10:20.220 --> 01:10:22.950
Country fried bison sounds strange.

01:10:22.950 --> 01:10:25.180
It was like country
style steak, but bison.

01:10:25.180 --> 01:10:26.040
So it was a little bit leaner.

01:10:26.040 --> 01:10:28.360
It was incredible.

01:10:28.360 --> 01:10:29.193
Wow.

01:10:30.030 --> 01:10:31.776
I highly recommend Savannah,

01:10:31.776 --> 01:10:32.609
and that's when it started to hit me.

01:10:32.609 --> 01:10:34.320
I was like, man, me and
Jesse would have a good time.

01:10:34.320 --> 01:10:36.027
Me and Jesse would have a better time

01:10:36.027 --> 01:10:37.020
than me and the boys are having here

01:10:37.020 --> 01:10:38.790
because it's got that sort of like,

01:10:38.790 --> 01:10:40.020
there's a little bit
of romance in the air.

01:10:40.020 --> 01:10:43.170
It's supposedly the most
haunted city in America.

01:10:43.170 --> 01:10:46.030
So if you're in like the
ghost tour of it all,

01:10:46.030 --> 01:10:48.669
there's a bunch of people going around

01:10:48.669 --> 01:10:52.037
in like, on a ghost tour basically.

01:10:52.037 --> 01:10:53.690
Oh, okay.

01:10:53.690 --> 01:10:55.140
Just so much history there.

01:10:56.940 --> 01:10:59.040
And then we ended up spending the night

01:10:59.040 --> 01:11:00.260
in Jacksonville, Florida.

01:11:00.260 --> 01:11:01.470
So we got all the way to Florida,

01:11:01.470 --> 01:11:02.310
from North Carolina to Florida.

01:11:02.310 --> 01:11:05.380
Again, it's like so much more time passed.

01:11:05.380 --> 01:11:07.500
Oh wow.
Or much more mileage

01:11:07.500 --> 01:11:09.630
under our belts in the
second half of the trip.

01:11:09.630 --> 01:11:10.790
We spend the night in Jacksonville,

01:11:10.790 --> 01:11:15.790
which by the way I learned
is the city by area

01:11:16.300 --> 01:11:18.293
in the continental United States.

01:11:19.450 --> 01:11:21.893
It's 840 square miles.

01:11:23.260 --> 01:11:25.594
Jacksonville, Florida is 840 square miles.

01:11:25.594 --> 01:11:27.297
Is that on the coast?

01:11:27.297 --> 01:11:28.130
Yeah.

01:11:28.130 --> 01:11:30.900
It's on the east coast of
Florida at the very top.

01:11:30.900 --> 01:11:32.330
Basically right when you get into Florida.

01:11:32.330 --> 01:11:33.163
Oh, okay.

01:11:33.163 --> 01:11:34.740
Yeah, I've only have passed through there,

01:11:34.740 --> 01:11:36.990
but I guess it did take a while.

01:11:36.990 --> 01:11:38.800
Don't feel like I gave
Jacksonville its due.

01:11:38.800 --> 01:11:40.760
We just got to a hotel late
at night and stayed there

01:11:40.760 --> 01:11:42.863
and then got a latte and got out.

01:11:44.878 --> 01:11:45.711
The latte was pretty good.

01:11:45.711 --> 01:11:48.940
But we wanted to get to St. Augustine.

01:11:48.940 --> 01:11:50.940
Oh, the oldest city in America.

01:11:50.940 --> 01:11:53.750
Yeah, so St. Augustine was.
My dad's been there

01:11:53.750 --> 01:11:54.583
a few times.

01:11:54.583 --> 01:11:57.160
He always talks about how
much he loves it down there.

01:11:57.160 --> 01:11:58.130
The grass!

01:11:58.130 --> 01:12:00.947
I could totally see your dad loving it,

01:12:00.947 --> 01:12:03.590
and I gotta say the sort of
middle aged white dude in me

01:12:03.590 --> 01:12:04.440
kind of loves it too.

01:12:04.440 --> 01:12:07.252
Like there's something
that kind of draws you.

01:12:07.252 --> 01:12:08.085
There's old houses,

01:12:08.085 --> 01:12:08.920
there's golf,

01:12:08.920 --> 01:12:10.000
there's grass.

01:12:10.000 --> 01:12:10.870
Yeah.
You should see

01:12:10.870 --> 01:12:12.477
the blades of grass down there.

01:12:12.477 --> 01:12:13.930
And the food in all these places,

01:12:13.930 --> 01:12:15.180
you're getting in just to like

01:12:15.180 --> 01:12:17.520
the best food in America, you know?

01:12:17.520 --> 01:12:19.740
I mean in St. Augustine,
you're kind of getting,

01:12:19.740 --> 01:12:22.890
so St. Augustine has a Spanish influence.

01:12:22.890 --> 01:12:25.430
I learned so much history.

01:12:25.430 --> 01:12:28.703
I didn't understand how
old St. Augustine was.

01:12:29.560 --> 01:12:31.630
But they basically like
built that mission there

01:12:31.630 --> 01:12:33.840
like a few decades after Columbus,

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:36.340
like we're talking like
1530 or something like that.

01:12:36.340 --> 01:12:37.930
They're basically like,
this is a town now,

01:12:37.930 --> 01:12:38.860
Spaniards.

01:12:38.860 --> 01:12:41.630
And then I also didn't
realize that the Spaniards

01:12:41.630 --> 01:12:45.250
owned Florida until the 1800s.

01:12:45.250 --> 01:12:46.083
Did you know that?

01:12:46.083 --> 01:12:47.820
And maybe the late 1800s.

01:12:47.820 --> 01:12:49.190
It's weird.

01:12:49.190 --> 01:12:50.440
I just didn't understand.

01:12:51.340 --> 01:12:52.680
We didn't take great history

01:12:52.680 --> 01:12:53.670
and I didn't pay attention to it.

01:12:53.670 --> 01:12:56.010
Well, you got the Louisiana Purchase.

01:12:56.010 --> 01:12:58.570
Yeah, that was when
Louisiana was purchased.

01:12:58.570 --> 01:12:59.640
We're talking about Florida.

01:12:59.640 --> 01:13:01.810
And then Florida was after that?

01:13:01.810 --> 01:13:05.810
Florida was like one of the
last stands on the East Coast.

01:13:05.810 --> 01:13:07.410
It was the last thing on the East Coast.

01:13:07.410 --> 01:13:08.620
Hmm.
There's a weird thing

01:13:08.620 --> 01:13:09.470
that happened with Key West

01:13:09.470 --> 01:13:11.480
and like the Confederacy at some point,

01:13:11.480 --> 01:13:16.230
and the Confederacy used the
Fort there at St. Augustine,

01:13:16.230 --> 01:13:18.673
which we toured at some
point during the war.

01:13:19.800 --> 01:13:20.950
I don't know, man.

01:13:20.950 --> 01:13:21.880
It's a cool place.

01:13:21.880 --> 01:13:23.390
There's a lot of history there,

01:13:23.390 --> 01:13:26.430
but we were heading to Miami,

01:13:26.430 --> 01:13:28.490
and this is when we started.

01:13:28.490 --> 01:13:31.100
We actually had been listening
to this ahead of time,

01:13:31.100 --> 01:13:31.980
but I'm gonna go out.

01:13:31.980 --> 01:13:32.813
I'm gonna break this out.

01:13:32.813 --> 01:13:34.470
This is my reck for the week.

01:13:34.470 --> 01:13:35.320
Okay.

01:13:35.320 --> 01:13:40.169
Have you heard Keith
Whitley's "Miami, Miami?"

01:13:40.169 --> 01:13:41.002
Yeah.
Which is

01:13:41.002 --> 01:13:42.540
his number one song.

01:13:42.540 --> 01:13:44.457
It's actually bigger than

01:13:44.457 --> 01:13:45.380
"When You Say It Best

01:13:45.380 --> 01:13:46.580
You Say Nothing At All."

01:13:48.652 --> 01:13:49.940
I don't know it that well,

01:13:49.940 --> 01:13:53.077
but I know that it
sounds like he's saying,

01:13:53.077 --> 01:13:54.130
"Miami, my Amy."

01:13:54.130 --> 01:13:57.033
Miami, my Amy.
Is gone or something.

01:13:58.130 --> 01:13:59.490
He thought was gonna be gone,

01:13:59.490 --> 01:14:00.930
but she, he's in LA.

01:14:00.930 --> 01:14:01.763
The thing is,

01:14:01.763 --> 01:14:03.400
is that he's in LA and she's in Miami

01:14:03.400 --> 01:14:06.383
and he's on a plane to Miami to see her.

01:14:07.631 --> 01:14:10.290
And Miami, my Amy loves me after all.

01:14:10.290 --> 01:14:12.670
It is an amazing song.

01:14:12.670 --> 01:14:14.580
We listened to a lot of Keith Whitley.

01:14:14.580 --> 01:14:15.830
We got into, you know.

01:14:15.830 --> 01:14:16.663
Tear jerker.

01:14:16.663 --> 01:14:17.675
He died.

01:14:17.675 --> 01:14:18.925
He died at 34.
Yeah.

01:14:20.500 --> 01:14:21.900
We listened to a lot of Keith Whitley.

01:14:21.900 --> 01:14:23.650
The boys were really into Keith Whitley.

01:14:23.650 --> 01:14:25.540
I wonder what he died from.

01:14:25.540 --> 01:14:27.750
Locke said alcoholism,

01:14:27.750 --> 01:14:29.300
when he looked it up on Wiki.

01:14:29.300 --> 01:14:30.330
That's sad.

01:14:30.330 --> 01:14:32.973
So we started playing like, Miami,

01:14:34.550 --> 01:14:36.530
like as you're driving towards Miami

01:14:36.530 --> 01:14:37.550
and you're playing Keith Whitley,

01:14:37.550 --> 01:14:39.440
it's just, everything
is falling into place.

01:14:39.440 --> 01:14:43.233
Much better than the Will
Smith, "Miami" route.

01:14:44.660 --> 01:14:45.493
Yeah.

01:14:45.493 --> 01:14:47.510
Still a fan, but I never liked that song.

01:14:47.510 --> 01:14:48.630
Well, speaking of songs,

01:14:48.630 --> 01:14:52.230
I talked about this in the
AMA that we did the other day

01:14:52.230 --> 01:14:53.230
for the Mythical Society,

01:14:53.230 --> 01:14:55.440
but I love to play music that is regional,

01:14:55.440 --> 01:14:58.170
whether it's, you know, if
we're heading through a town.

01:14:58.170 --> 01:15:01.040
So like Dawes has a great
song, "St. Augustine At Night,"

01:15:01.040 --> 01:15:02.440
which we listened to in the morning

01:15:02.440 --> 01:15:03.623
because that's when we
were in St. Augustine.

01:15:03.623 --> 01:15:04.820
Hmm.
We kind of hated

01:15:04.820 --> 01:15:05.653
that we weren't there at night,

01:15:05.653 --> 01:15:07.050
but that's a great song.

01:15:07.050 --> 01:15:08.343
But listening to Miami,

01:15:09.270 --> 01:15:12.600
and then we're heading into Miami

01:15:12.600 --> 01:15:14.433
but I had a little surprise planned,

01:15:15.724 --> 01:15:19.320
and that was to stop and take
a fan boat on the Everglades.

01:15:19.320 --> 01:15:20.290
No!

01:15:20.290 --> 01:15:22.360
Really?

01:15:22.360 --> 01:15:23.470
Oh, you did that?

01:15:23.470 --> 01:15:24.740
And I did it.

01:15:24.740 --> 01:15:26.170
Oh man!

01:15:26.170 --> 01:15:27.003
I'm so jealous.

01:15:27.003 --> 01:15:29.500
That's the one thing that I've
wanted to do before I die!

01:15:29.500 --> 01:15:30.427
Yeah, well, I've always wanted to.

01:15:30.427 --> 01:15:32.060
Now you can die.
I've always wanted

01:15:32.060 --> 01:15:33.440
to be on a fan boat,

01:15:33.440 --> 01:15:35.620
and I did.
So you made plans.

01:15:35.620 --> 01:15:36.453
I made plans.

01:15:36.453 --> 01:15:37.730
Okay.
And I almost

01:15:37.730 --> 01:15:39.103
didn't get there in time.

01:15:40.020 --> 01:15:43.360
Man, so the Everglades are really big

01:15:43.360 --> 01:15:44.860
and they start pretty high,

01:15:44.860 --> 01:15:47.920
but they end in the bottom in Miami.

01:15:47.920 --> 01:15:49.990
So you're gonna take a fan boat to Miami?

01:15:49.990 --> 01:15:51.156
No.

01:15:51.156 --> 01:15:53.130
So I was basically, there
was multiple locations,

01:15:53.130 --> 01:15:54.185
but I was like,

01:15:54.185 --> 01:15:56.830
I'm gonna drive to the
one that's next to Miami.

01:15:56.830 --> 01:15:58.730
So we're kind of like
almost done with our day

01:15:58.730 --> 01:16:01.490
and we do this like late
afternoon fan boat ride.

01:16:01.490 --> 01:16:02.680
Oh, okay.

01:16:02.680 --> 01:16:06.550
And shout out to the people

01:16:06.550 --> 01:16:08.940
at Saw Grass Recreational Center.

01:16:08.940 --> 01:16:11.700
They have a little reptile zoo

01:16:11.700 --> 01:16:14.010
and then they have the fan boat rides,

01:16:14.010 --> 01:16:17.693
and they're a few Mythical
Beasts on the staff there.

01:16:19.770 --> 01:16:21.673
And we,

01:16:22.550 --> 01:16:23.510
I learned some things.

01:16:23.510 --> 01:16:24.953
So we get, it's just the three of us,

01:16:24.953 --> 01:16:26.780
and the dude operating the fan boat.

01:16:26.780 --> 01:16:27.870
How many seats were on there?

01:16:27.870 --> 01:16:28.880
Six?

01:16:28.880 --> 01:16:33.550
There's three rows of bench
seats with no seat belts.

01:16:33.550 --> 01:16:34.383
Oh?

01:16:34.383 --> 01:16:36.004
No, there's nothing over you.

01:16:36.004 --> 01:16:39.360
It's just like a bench and
you just gotta hold on.

01:16:39.360 --> 01:16:40.627
And so Locke and Shepherd
sat in front of me

01:16:40.627 --> 01:16:42.500
and I sat behind them.

01:16:42.500 --> 01:16:43.550
It's super loud.

01:16:43.550 --> 01:16:45.140
They give you earplugs.

01:16:45.140 --> 01:16:46.420
Oh, we didn't wear 'em 'cause you know,

01:16:46.420 --> 01:16:47.270
we're in Florida.

01:16:48.720 --> 01:16:49.553
We'll lose our hearing.

01:16:49.553 --> 01:16:50.639
It's fine.

01:16:50.639 --> 01:16:52.490
And then it's super loud,

01:16:52.490 --> 01:16:54.620
but he's like, man,

01:16:54.620 --> 01:16:56.370
he's lived there all his life

01:16:56.370 --> 01:16:57.370
and he's explaining.

01:16:57.370 --> 01:17:00.040
I'm like, "So, do you guys have like?"

01:17:00.040 --> 01:17:01.540
You know, first of all,
we're gonna see some gators.

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:02.900
He was like, "Yes, we're
gonna see some gators."

01:17:02.900 --> 01:17:03.733
And we did.

01:17:03.733 --> 01:17:05.016
And he was like,

01:17:05.016 --> 01:17:05.849
"They're active at this time of the day."

01:17:05.849 --> 01:17:07.320
"It's mating season."

01:17:07.320 --> 01:17:08.670
So we probably saw.

01:17:08.670 --> 01:17:12.480
Gators mating?
12 alligators in the wild,

01:17:12.480 --> 01:17:13.773
some really big ones.

01:17:15.270 --> 01:17:17.600
And then he was explaining, no, he did.

01:17:17.600 --> 01:17:19.346
We said, have you ever seen 'em mating?

01:17:19.346 --> 01:17:20.179
He was like, "Just the other day

01:17:20.179 --> 01:17:22.155
they were mating when I showed up

01:17:22.155 --> 01:17:23.280
and they rolled into the water."

01:17:23.280 --> 01:17:26.430
We didn't get to see any
gator sex as much as I wanted.

01:17:26.430 --> 01:17:28.513
I think they charge extra if you see that.

01:17:30.000 --> 01:17:32.656
It's like a little
retroactive thing, but then.

01:17:32.656 --> 01:17:34.659
It's like putting a
foot into a gator boot.

01:17:34.659 --> 01:17:36.360
And then,

01:17:36.360 --> 01:17:37.500
but I'm asking him about,

01:17:37.500 --> 01:17:41.303
I'm just kind of noticing
that it's all water.

01:17:42.190 --> 01:17:43.900
I mean, yes, there's a,

01:17:43.900 --> 01:17:45.530
it's like two feet of
water and there's a whole,

01:17:45.530 --> 01:17:46.810
it looks like land,

01:17:46.810 --> 01:17:49.140
but I can kind of tell
that it's all water.

01:17:49.140 --> 01:17:51.180
There's no like, land.

01:17:51.180 --> 01:17:52.013
And he's like, "Yeah,

01:17:52.013 --> 01:17:55.190
the bottom half of the
Everglades is 600 square miles."

01:17:55.190 --> 01:17:57.288
Not as big as Jacksonville.

01:17:57.288 --> 01:17:59.610
Maybe it's 6,000 square miles.

01:17:59.610 --> 01:18:00.443
That makes more sense.

01:18:00.443 --> 01:18:02.490
Oh, so you didn't have
any like the Cypress trees

01:18:02.490 --> 01:18:03.323
or whatever those trees.
Right,

01:18:03.323 --> 01:18:05.670
he says, "That's the land

01:18:05.670 --> 01:18:07.090
that's gonna have some trees and stuff,

01:18:07.090 --> 01:18:07.987
it's further north."

01:18:07.987 --> 01:18:09.322
And he says,

01:18:09.322 --> 01:18:10.510
"And that's where all those
invasive pythons are."

01:18:10.510 --> 01:18:11.860
The ones that have like taken over

01:18:11.860 --> 01:18:12.693
the whole Everglades

01:18:12.693 --> 01:18:14.301
because of people
letting their snakes out.

01:18:14.301 --> 01:18:15.320
Wow.
Their pet snakes.

01:18:15.320 --> 01:18:17.140
He said, "I've only
seen one down here ever

01:18:17.140 --> 01:18:18.940
because they want some land."

01:18:18.940 --> 01:18:21.440
But then he likes telling us
about the history of this area.

01:18:21.440 --> 01:18:24.100
'Cause there's also this
invasive species of bird.

01:18:24.100 --> 01:18:25.910
He was like, "Yeah, like in the nineties,

01:18:25.910 --> 01:18:28.310
there was a hurricane and this bird

01:18:28.310 --> 01:18:29.670
that's from another place in the world

01:18:29.670 --> 01:18:30.940
got out from some zoo,

01:18:30.940 --> 01:18:31.997
and now they all live around there

01:18:31.997 --> 01:18:34.052
and the gators love to eat 'em."

01:18:34.052 --> 01:18:34.885
And so like,

01:18:34.885 --> 01:18:36.830
it's just cool to cool
to see like the ecosystem

01:18:36.830 --> 01:18:39.091
and how, I guess how screwed it up it is.

01:18:39.091 --> 01:18:40.441
Were there paths?

01:18:40.441 --> 01:18:42.363
Because there was reeds everywhere,

01:18:42.363 --> 01:18:44.390
with paths, right?
We were going through

01:18:44.390 --> 01:18:46.980
some obviously natural sort of canals,

01:18:46.980 --> 01:18:49.200
but there was one really
long, straight canal.

01:18:49.200 --> 01:18:50.620
I was like, "Now this
isn't natural, right?"

01:18:50.620 --> 01:18:51.453
He says, "No,

01:18:51.453 --> 01:18:53.890
actually a hundred years ago or so

01:18:53.890 --> 01:18:55.020
the Army Corps of Engineers

01:18:55.020 --> 01:18:58.970
decided that they were going to drain this

01:18:58.970 --> 01:19:00.397
and put cows here."

01:19:01.472 --> 01:19:03.350
And so they put the cows out here

01:19:03.350 --> 01:19:05.870
and it was an absolute disaster

01:19:05.870 --> 01:19:07.270
because it was like super muddy

01:19:07.270 --> 01:19:09.770
and they couldn't keep
pumping the water out.

01:19:09.770 --> 01:19:12.680
And so then they just let it fill back up.

01:19:12.680 --> 01:19:14.580
And but one of the things that happened

01:19:14.580 --> 01:19:18.040
is there are these little
tiny pockets of land.

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:21.050
Like, I'm talking like big
enough to put a house on,

01:19:21.050 --> 01:19:23.760
that people could come out and claim.

01:19:23.760 --> 01:19:25.590
So he took us to this island

01:19:26.580 --> 01:19:29.430
and there was this island
that we didn't go on,

01:19:29.430 --> 01:19:30.263
like, we drove,

01:19:30.263 --> 01:19:31.173
we rode up to it,

01:19:32.018 --> 01:19:33.640
and it was this house on this island.

01:19:33.640 --> 01:19:36.585
What?
With this giant Trump flag,

01:19:36.585 --> 01:19:37.880
of course,

01:19:37.880 --> 01:19:40.260
like, this is Trump country,

01:19:40.260 --> 01:19:42.623
and this guy has just set up his,

01:19:44.520 --> 01:19:45.880
he lives out there full time.

01:19:45.880 --> 01:19:46.713
Off the grid.

01:19:46.713 --> 01:19:47.840
No power, of course.

01:19:47.840 --> 01:19:48.673
Off the grid.

01:19:48.673 --> 01:19:50.580
He has his own, like, I
think he's got solar panels

01:19:50.580 --> 01:19:51.600
or something,

01:19:51.600 --> 01:19:53.000
but like, it's just his land

01:19:53.000 --> 01:19:53.833
'cause you could,

01:19:53.833 --> 01:19:55.677
at some point in the past
you could just claim it

01:19:55.677 --> 01:19:57.160
and they got grandfathered in

01:19:57.160 --> 01:19:58.367
and never had to pay for it.

01:19:58.367 --> 01:20:00.837
And so he is like, "Yeah, this
guy is like 70 years old."

01:20:00.837 --> 01:20:03.027
"He's retired. He lives out here."

01:20:03.027 --> 01:20:06.420
"And he bought a fan boat from us,"

01:20:06.420 --> 01:20:08.400
from the place that I got the fan boats.

01:20:08.400 --> 01:20:10.930
And so he has this transportation
on his own fan boat

01:20:10.930 --> 01:20:13.081
that he goes back and forth on the land,

01:20:13.081 --> 01:20:13.914
just living out there.

01:20:13.914 --> 01:20:16.840
Completely isolated.
And surrounded by alligators

01:20:16.840 --> 01:20:17.760
by the way.

01:20:17.760 --> 01:20:19.593
Surrounded by them.

01:20:20.730 --> 01:20:21.859
Wow.

01:20:21.859 --> 01:20:23.090
So it's wild, man.

01:20:23.090 --> 01:20:26.120
So if we had to do it again,

01:20:26.120 --> 01:20:28.680
you'd be up to go to like
the northern Everglades

01:20:28.680 --> 01:20:30.920
'cause it's a different experience.

01:20:30.920 --> 01:20:31.753
Yeah.

01:20:31.753 --> 01:20:33.190
Where the woods are.

01:20:33.190 --> 01:20:34.332
Yeah.

01:20:34.332 --> 01:20:35.165
I mean, I'd also do that again.

01:20:35.165 --> 01:20:35.998
I mean,

01:20:37.677 --> 01:20:38.530
I don't know.

01:20:38.530 --> 01:20:40.150
I may be going back to Miami a lot.

01:20:40.150 --> 01:20:42.090
Locke hasn't made a decision about school,

01:20:42.090 --> 01:20:43.920
but that was the final destination

01:20:43.920 --> 01:20:46.510
was to tour the school that
he's interested in Miami,

01:20:46.510 --> 01:20:48.520
which we toured,

01:20:48.520 --> 01:20:49.853
and it was beautiful.

01:20:51.180 --> 01:20:54.250
But the last piece of the trip here is,

01:20:54.250 --> 01:20:55.350
so we,

01:20:55.350 --> 01:20:58.863
oh, that night we go,

01:21:00.260 --> 01:21:02.650
we went to Miami Beach.

01:21:02.650 --> 01:21:05.180
Which again, if you remember
the geography of Miami,

01:21:05.180 --> 01:21:07.830
there's a pretty giant downtown

01:21:07.830 --> 01:21:10.480
that's on the coast, right?

01:21:10.480 --> 01:21:13.530
And then there's an island
that is out from the coast,

01:21:13.530 --> 01:21:15.160
which is where the real beach is.

01:21:15.160 --> 01:21:17.430
And that's like where we
stayed at the Fontainebleau

01:21:17.430 --> 01:21:18.710
and stuff like that
when we did that event.

01:21:18.710 --> 01:21:19.730
Okay, yeah.

01:21:19.730 --> 01:21:20.800
And that's where like the beach is

01:21:20.800 --> 01:21:23.860
because there's not much beach at all.

01:21:23.860 --> 01:21:25.010
'Cause it's pretty much mangroves

01:21:25.010 --> 01:21:28.260
that come all the way down
to the water in most places.

01:21:28.260 --> 01:21:30.460
So there's not really a shoreline

01:21:30.460 --> 01:21:31.787
except like a traditional beach,

01:21:31.787 --> 01:21:34.600
except Miami beach has a heck of a beach.

01:21:34.600 --> 01:21:37.747
So that's where we went, you know,

01:21:37.747 --> 01:21:39.410
and we stayed in Miami Beach.

01:21:39.410 --> 01:21:41.400
Yeah.
We did the night swimming.

01:21:41.400 --> 01:21:42.560
Yeah.
So night swimming

01:21:42.560 --> 01:21:44.000
was on the agenda.

01:21:44.000 --> 01:21:45.430
Oh!

01:21:45.430 --> 01:21:48.530
Now we ate some incredible food.

01:21:48.530 --> 01:21:50.770
Miami has so good,

01:21:50.770 --> 01:21:52.100
the food is so good.

01:21:52.100 --> 01:21:53.100
We ate at this like,

01:21:54.054 --> 01:21:56.940
it was like Cuban, Peruvian
fusion or something.

01:21:56.940 --> 01:21:58.070
I don't know what it was, man,

01:21:58.070 --> 01:22:01.350
but it was unbelievable.

01:22:01.350 --> 01:22:03.585
It may have been my favorite
meal of the whole trip.

01:22:03.585 --> 01:22:04.510
I mean the food on this trip,

01:22:04.510 --> 01:22:06.610
I just love eating so much.

01:22:06.610 --> 01:22:08.440
I should have a show on Food Network.

01:22:08.440 --> 01:22:10.607
So Miami, you enjoy it while you can

01:22:10.607 --> 01:22:12.150
before it all goes underwater.

01:22:12.150 --> 01:22:14.930
Yeah, that's what I keep thinking

01:22:14.930 --> 01:22:15.763
when I see it.

01:22:15.763 --> 01:22:17.060
I'm like, man, you know,

01:22:18.400 --> 01:22:19.390
this is not gonna be around,

01:22:19.390 --> 01:22:22.340
but it's around now.

01:22:22.340 --> 01:22:24.590
And we go to the beach

01:22:24.590 --> 01:22:25.520
and I was like, "Guys,

01:22:25.520 --> 01:22:27.820
one of the best experiences
in my life is night swimming."

01:22:27.820 --> 01:22:30.310
And one of their favorite
songs is "Night Swimming."

01:22:30.310 --> 01:22:32.480
We have songs that are
like top to everything

01:22:32.480 --> 01:22:34.520
and then boys.
REM?

01:22:34.520 --> 01:22:35.353
Yeah.

01:22:35.353 --> 01:22:38.380
And so I was like, all right,

01:22:38.380 --> 01:22:39.213
we go down there,

01:22:39.213 --> 01:22:41.390
now I don't remember
what time of year it was

01:22:41.390 --> 01:22:45.100
when we night swam with
Stevie, and Ben, and Alex,

01:22:45.100 --> 01:22:47.630
and not Alex Punch, but Alex Alexandrof.

01:22:47.630 --> 01:22:48.784
Yeah.

01:22:48.784 --> 01:22:49.860
But it was hot.

01:22:49.860 --> 01:22:50.693
It was hot.

01:22:50.693 --> 01:22:52.010
And the water felt like a bath.

01:22:52.010 --> 01:22:53.500
And you couldn't tell the
difference in the temperature

01:22:53.500 --> 01:22:54.630
between the air and the water.

01:22:54.630 --> 01:22:59.630
Not so much the case in early
April when we were there.

01:23:00.080 --> 01:23:02.410
So Locke was scared of sharks

01:23:03.300 --> 01:23:06.640
and it didn't feel great.

01:23:06.640 --> 01:23:07.790
It felt great outside of the water,

01:23:07.790 --> 01:23:09.400
but it was kind of a little chilly,

01:23:09.400 --> 01:23:11.640
like when the bath's a little bit too cold

01:23:11.640 --> 01:23:12.473
kind of situation.

01:23:12.473 --> 01:23:13.990
Okay.
But I was like,

01:23:13.990 --> 01:23:15.230
you guys have to get in

01:23:15.230 --> 01:23:17.630
and sing at least the
first verse and chorus

01:23:17.630 --> 01:23:19.480
of "Night Swimming" and then get out.

01:23:20.700 --> 01:23:21.860
So they did that.

01:23:21.860 --> 01:23:25.533
And then that was the last,

01:23:26.390 --> 01:23:28.550
so we were what we were supposed to do,

01:23:28.550 --> 01:23:29.383
night swim,

01:23:29.383 --> 01:23:30.760
then go stay in our hotel,

01:23:30.760 --> 01:23:32.860
then tour the campus.

01:23:32.860 --> 01:23:34.470
Couldn't get an official tour,

01:23:34.470 --> 01:23:36.010
but this campus was an open campus

01:23:36.010 --> 01:23:38.820
so we, you know, we could tour it anyway.

01:23:38.820 --> 01:23:39.900
Official tours were like booked

01:23:39.900 --> 01:23:41.493
or not available on a Saturday.

01:23:43.250 --> 01:23:46.283
But there was a convergence of events,

01:23:47.560 --> 01:23:48.570
and that is

01:23:49.560 --> 01:23:52.220
that we knew that Carolina and Duke

01:23:52.220 --> 01:23:55.360
were playing each other in the final four.

01:23:55.360 --> 01:23:56.370
That night.

01:23:56.370 --> 01:23:57.827
That night, Saturday night.

01:23:57.827 --> 01:23:59.290
And this is the night that
we're supposed to come back,

01:23:59.290 --> 01:24:01.400
and I bought these
flights a long time ago.

01:24:01.400 --> 01:24:02.630
Now I'm super conflicted

01:24:02.630 --> 01:24:05.030
because I'm an NC State grad.

01:24:05.030 --> 01:24:06.220
I do not like Carolina.

01:24:06.220 --> 01:24:07.200
I do not like Duke.

01:24:07.200 --> 01:24:10.160
I do not like Carolina more
than I do not like Duke,

01:24:10.160 --> 01:24:12.533
but this is like watching,

01:24:13.400 --> 01:24:14.400
as I said on Twitter,

01:24:14.400 --> 01:24:16.650
the two popular bullies in school fight.

01:24:16.650 --> 01:24:18.110
It's like, I don't like either one of 'em,

01:24:18.110 --> 01:24:19.440
but I'm showing up for the fight.

01:24:19.440 --> 01:24:20.830
Yeah, even I watched it.

01:24:20.830 --> 01:24:21.700
Right.

01:24:21.700 --> 01:24:24.130
And if you don't have
any context for this,

01:24:24.130 --> 01:24:26.920
I mean, this was the most
important college basketball game

01:24:26.920 --> 01:24:28.510
in the history of college basketball.

01:24:28.510 --> 01:24:31.410
'Cause this is what might be
the biggest sports rivalry.

01:24:31.410 --> 01:24:33.810
I think was actually voted
the biggest sports rivalry

01:24:33.810 --> 01:24:37.080
over the Red Sox and the Yankees

01:24:37.080 --> 01:24:38.470
by Sports Illustrated back in the day.

01:24:38.470 --> 01:24:39.303
I don't know about that,

01:24:39.303 --> 01:24:41.453
but it is for us the biggest thing.

01:24:42.390 --> 01:24:44.110
And they were gonna be playing

01:24:44.110 --> 01:24:46.380
literally at the same time
that we were on the plane,

01:24:46.380 --> 01:24:47.360
and getting off of a plane,

01:24:47.360 --> 01:24:48.430
and getting onto another plane.

01:24:48.430 --> 01:24:49.570
And I was like,

01:24:49.570 --> 01:24:52.100
Locke is a huge fan of basketball

01:24:52.100 --> 01:24:53.840
and he really wants to watch this game.

01:24:53.840 --> 01:24:54.817
I'm a huge fan of basketball

01:24:54.817 --> 01:24:57.400
and kind of want to
begrudgingly watch this game.

01:24:57.400 --> 01:24:58.920
So you pulled an Eric Church.

01:24:58.920 --> 01:25:00.203
I changed the flight.

01:25:01.050 --> 01:25:03.017
I was like, we're gonna watch this

01:25:03.017 --> 01:25:05.270
and we're gonna fly out in the morning.

01:25:05.270 --> 01:25:07.263
So yeah, we,

01:25:08.444 --> 01:25:09.277
this wasn't,

01:25:09.277 --> 01:25:11.540
didn't have quite the
repercussions of Eric Church's.

01:25:11.540 --> 01:25:13.889
Barbara, quit scratching over there!

01:25:13.889 --> 01:25:15.306
What you doing?

01:25:15.306 --> 01:25:16.139
Why you gotta be a troublemaker?

01:25:16.139 --> 01:25:16.972
Why can't you be like Jade

01:25:16.972 --> 01:25:18.790
and just sit in my lap the whole time?

01:25:24.030 --> 01:25:27.360
So, we changed the flight.

01:25:27.360 --> 01:25:28.503
We watched the game.

01:25:29.443 --> 01:25:30.276
We watched the game,

01:25:30.276 --> 01:25:32.560
we watched the first game, the Villanova,

01:25:32.560 --> 01:25:34.070
not the Villanova,

01:25:34.070 --> 01:25:34.903
whatever the first game was.

01:25:34.903 --> 01:25:35.736
I've already forgotten it.

01:25:35.736 --> 01:25:36.580
It was Kansas

01:25:37.720 --> 01:25:40.128
and whoever they beat.

01:25:40.128 --> 01:25:41.360
I can't remember.
And watched

01:25:41.360 --> 01:25:44.113
that first game in a bar.

01:25:46.040 --> 01:25:47.500
And then we were like,

01:25:47.500 --> 01:25:48.850
the second game, we're gonna watch it

01:25:48.850 --> 01:25:51.420
in the comfort of our hotel room

01:25:51.420 --> 01:25:52.870
so we can get as loud as we want,

01:25:52.870 --> 01:25:54.260
even though we're not really,

01:25:54.260 --> 01:25:55.093
I wasn't,

01:25:55.093 --> 01:25:56.410
Locke was pulling for Carolina.

01:25:57.840 --> 01:25:58.956
You were,

01:25:58.956 --> 01:25:59.890
you better be pulling for Duke.

01:25:59.890 --> 01:26:03.317
So, this very interesting
thing happened to me,

01:26:03.317 --> 01:26:05.983
and I talked about this.
I saw your tweet tweets.

01:26:05.983 --> 01:26:07.270
I was like, what's going on here?

01:26:07.270 --> 01:26:08.933
Well, here's the thing,

01:26:11.590 --> 01:26:12.793
I've lost some of my,

01:26:14.460 --> 01:26:16.720
I think that one of the things is,

01:26:16.720 --> 01:26:18.200
I, for many years now

01:26:18.200 --> 01:26:22.770
I've been able to step
outside of the real passion

01:26:22.770 --> 01:26:25.280
and emotion that I have around sports

01:26:25.280 --> 01:26:27.823
and understand that it is fake passion.

01:26:28.690 --> 01:26:31.990
That it is the way I feel about sports

01:26:31.990 --> 01:26:33.110
and the competitiveness of like,

01:26:33.110 --> 01:26:35.360
if NC State's playing somebody,

01:26:35.360 --> 01:26:36.400
the way I feel,

01:26:36.400 --> 01:26:39.300
like my brain in my body

01:26:39.300 --> 01:26:41.390
doesn't know the difference between

01:26:41.390 --> 01:26:44.673
a life or death situation
and NC State sports.

01:26:44.673 --> 01:26:47.040
That's what sports is like

01:26:47.040 --> 01:26:48.874
for someone who really cares about it.

01:26:48.874 --> 01:26:50.370
Yeah.
And I understand

01:26:50.370 --> 01:26:51.820
from an outsider perspective,

01:26:51.820 --> 01:26:53.110
that seems crazy

01:26:53.110 --> 01:26:56.270
because it is definitively crazy

01:26:56.270 --> 01:26:58.080
to care that much

01:26:58.080 --> 01:27:01.010
to the point that years
are coming off your life.

01:27:01.010 --> 01:27:02.700
You're doing things that
are bad for your health.

01:27:02.700 --> 01:27:04.280
Maybe getting yourself into a situation

01:27:04.280 --> 01:27:05.760
where violence happens,

01:27:05.760 --> 01:27:08.560
because to other people
or maybe to a wall,

01:27:08.560 --> 01:27:10.983
because you care so much about this stuff.

01:27:12.430 --> 01:27:14.440
And something about being in LA

01:27:14.440 --> 01:27:16.423
and also my life filling up
with so many other things

01:27:16.423 --> 01:27:17.820
that like, I'm just like,

01:27:17.820 --> 01:27:18.830
I still care about this,

01:27:18.830 --> 01:27:21.500
but it's really hard
to get passionate about

01:27:21.500 --> 01:27:22.930
NC State athletics

01:27:22.930 --> 01:27:25.440
when it tends to be just a marathon

01:27:25.440 --> 01:27:28.450
or merry go round of disappointment
a lot of times, right?

01:27:28.450 --> 01:27:31.050
So, and especially when
it comes to basketball

01:27:31.050 --> 01:27:33.450
and you live in the shadow
of Duke and Carolina.

01:27:34.810 --> 01:27:36.610
All these people in my
family go to Carolina.

01:27:36.610 --> 01:27:37.443
My wife went to Carolina.

01:27:37.443 --> 01:27:38.730
My brother goes to Carolina.

01:27:39.730 --> 01:27:41.240
My nephew goes to Carolina.

01:27:41.240 --> 01:27:43.933
Locke wanted to go to
Carolina, but he's not.

01:27:46.410 --> 01:27:49.610
And so, and I was also like,

01:27:49.610 --> 01:27:52.110
I wanna see Coach K go out with a win

01:27:52.110 --> 01:27:54.110
because I used to be a Duke
fan when I was in high school

01:27:54.110 --> 01:27:55.564
for no other reason than

01:27:55.564 --> 01:27:57.310
I just like picked a team
'cause they were good.

01:27:57.310 --> 01:28:00.690
And I was like, I'd like to
see Coach K go out with a win.

01:28:00.690 --> 01:28:02.430
But then you just kind of look at this

01:28:02.430 --> 01:28:04.840
hobbling, North Carolina team

01:28:04.840 --> 01:28:06.480
where they've got seven guys who play.

01:28:06.480 --> 01:28:08.460
They got this new coach, Hubert Davis,

01:28:08.460 --> 01:28:10.200
who I like as a person.

01:28:10.200 --> 01:28:11.450
They're playing hurt.

01:28:11.450 --> 01:28:13.230
They're an eight seed.

01:28:13.230 --> 01:28:15.320
It's just like, it was
hard not to pull for them,

01:28:15.320 --> 01:28:17.190
but in the end I was,

01:28:17.190 --> 01:28:20.530
I got to a place where I was
literally 100% indifferent

01:28:20.530 --> 01:28:21.760
to the outcome,

01:28:21.760 --> 01:28:24.120
but still nervous at the same time.

01:28:24.120 --> 01:28:26.870
I had this weird, anxious
energy watching it.

01:28:26.870 --> 01:28:28.453
And I was like,

01:28:28.453 --> 01:28:29.470
you know, if Carolina wins this game,

01:28:29.470 --> 01:28:32.330
it'll be crazy.

01:28:32.330 --> 01:28:35.090
If Coach K wins this
game, it'll be fitting,

01:28:35.090 --> 01:28:38.520
and like, he'll go out on a high note.

01:28:38.520 --> 01:28:40.970
And so in the end when Carolina won,

01:28:40.970 --> 01:28:44.123
I just kind of felt just
an empty nothingness.

01:28:44.123 --> 01:28:44.956
And I was like,

01:28:44.956 --> 01:28:48.030
I changed my flight for nothingness.

01:28:48.030 --> 01:28:49.680
I altered in my entire schedule.

01:28:49.680 --> 01:28:51.640
I paid extra money so I can watch

01:28:51.640 --> 01:28:56.640
an emotionally, just non-gratifying.

01:28:57.040 --> 01:28:57.873
You know what I'm saying?

01:28:57.873 --> 01:28:59.120
It was just such a weird thing.

01:28:59.120 --> 01:29:02.090
I was like, I'm so glad I
watched this historic game,

01:29:02.090 --> 01:29:03.680
but I got nothing from it.

01:29:03.680 --> 01:29:06.650
It was like eating a burger

01:29:06.650 --> 01:29:08.882
that's made out newspaper.

01:29:08.882 --> 01:29:10.160
Eh.

01:29:10.160 --> 01:29:14.110
I don't end your entire
trip account on that front.

01:29:14.110 --> 01:29:15.660
I mean, why don't you
end on something else?

01:29:15.660 --> 01:29:17.070
Well, that was the end of the trip.

01:29:17.070 --> 01:29:18.820
But let me just say,

01:29:18.820 --> 01:29:20.720
we had an incredible time.

01:29:20.720 --> 01:29:22.260
Some of the, you know, I couldn't,

01:29:22.260 --> 01:29:24.320
I've already gone for an hour and a half,

01:29:24.320 --> 01:29:28.330
but some of the conversations
that we got into,

01:29:28.330 --> 01:29:29.770
like we got so tickled.

01:29:29.770 --> 01:29:33.123
Like, now with Shepard being 13,

01:29:34.030 --> 01:29:35.980
the nature of the conversations

01:29:35.980 --> 01:29:37.810
and the funny things that we talk about

01:29:37.810 --> 01:29:40.110
and the way that we make each other laugh,

01:29:40.110 --> 01:29:44.040
it's just, I really value that
time that I have with them.

01:29:44.040 --> 01:29:44.873
We just have a good time.

01:29:44.873 --> 01:29:46.370
You're all on a 13 year old level.

01:29:46.370 --> 01:29:47.203
Yeah, right.

01:29:47.203 --> 01:29:49.270
It's like, we're all
now on the same level.

01:29:49.270 --> 01:29:50.103
And so,

01:29:51.778 --> 01:29:53.660
boy, we just talked about so much weird,

01:29:53.660 --> 01:29:57.670
philosophical, existential, funny stuff,

01:29:57.670 --> 01:29:59.810
and just, you know, they're
both very passionate

01:29:59.810 --> 01:30:02.060
and have a bunch of
opinions about everything,

01:30:02.060 --> 01:30:04.340
just like somebody they're related to,

01:30:04.340 --> 01:30:06.300
or two people they're related to.

01:30:06.300 --> 01:30:08.660
And I just had the best time.

01:30:08.660 --> 01:30:10.340
And Locke and I, we,

01:30:10.340 --> 01:30:11.173
the funny thing is,

01:30:11.173 --> 01:30:12.770
is Locke and I had such a great time,

01:30:12.770 --> 01:30:15.800
and then the very first
night when Shepherd shows up,

01:30:15.800 --> 01:30:17.273
Locke's like, Shepherd
was doing something,

01:30:17.273 --> 01:30:18.960
like he was getting in the shower

01:30:18.960 --> 01:30:20.539
and he like left something.

01:30:20.539 --> 01:30:21.460
I was like, "Don't do
that with the towel."

01:30:21.460 --> 01:30:22.447
And Locke was like,

01:30:22.447 --> 01:30:24.437
"Shepherd, don't screw up our system."

01:30:24.437 --> 01:30:27.410
"Dad and I have a system
that we have established

01:30:27.410 --> 01:30:28.990
and you can't be messing up the system."

01:30:28.990 --> 01:30:31.130
I was like, I'm proud of him.

01:30:31.130 --> 01:30:32.440
I didn't even tell him it was a system,

01:30:32.440 --> 01:30:35.190
but he picked up on the
fact that we had a system.

01:30:35.190 --> 01:30:36.560
We were in a rhythm.
You used

01:30:36.560 --> 01:30:37.610
the top half of the towel.

01:30:37.610 --> 01:30:38.700
He used the bottom half of the towel?

01:30:38.700 --> 01:30:39.540
Well, yep.

01:30:39.540 --> 01:30:42.190
Well, usually there's two,

01:30:42.190 --> 01:30:43.023
but yeah,

01:30:43.023 --> 01:30:44.210
Shepherd was,

01:30:44.210 --> 01:30:46.360
Shepherd was invading on
the system a little bit,

01:30:46.360 --> 01:30:48.510
but he eventually fell in line.

01:30:48.510 --> 01:30:50.833
Well, I'm glad you guys
had that, you know?

01:30:50.833 --> 01:30:52.460
It's something you'll never forget.

01:30:52.460 --> 01:30:54.970
And if you do, just
listen to this podcast.

01:30:54.970 --> 01:30:59.260
